#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Run the required checks on this machine and sign what they measured. Nobody has to ask for a signature.

  scripts/fractal-test [--check NAME]... [--rerun] [--base origin/main] [--push REMOTE | --out DIR] [--attempt N] [--time-limit SECONDS] [--queue-limit SECONDS]
  scripts/fractal-test --seed [REF] [--base origin/main] [--push REMOTE | --out DIR]
  scripts/fractal-test --list
  scripts/fractal-test --status [--base origin/main]

For every check that has a local command (the table below: the whole per-PR suite; run `scripts/fractal-test --push origin` on the head
you mark ready, and --all, accepted, means the same) it runs that command once, on the tree the pull request will be tested as, through scripts/evidence-wrap, and the signer records the exit code and the digest of
the output it measured under this host's key (scripts/evidence-keygen). A failing run is recorded too. It then
asks the verifier, from the accepted base, whether the tree is now covered, and prints one line per check.

The tree: HEAD, the commit you are pushing. The CI gate looks up the note for the pull request head's own tree, so the tree
you built and signed is the tree that counts even when main has moved since; the merge queue still tests the integrated tree.
When the gate would judge the merge instead (the branch is more than HEAD_TREE_MAX_BEHIND commits behind, or protected files moved on
main since the branch point), a signing run first merges the base into the branch here, as a new head commit, and signs that head; with
--push it then pushes the branch at that merge (merge_base_in, push_branch; docs/st3/gate-evidence.md).
A check this tree already has a passing signature for (the verifier's answer, the one asked after a run) is not run again: it
prints `already signed`, so re-running on an unchanged tree is near instant (with --push its note is pushed as it is, so
the remote has it too); --rerun runs it anyway. It tells you how far behind the base the branch is.
The command always runs in a clean worktree of that commit, never
in this checkout, so untracked or ignored files here cannot change the result. The worktree is one of a small pool of persistent
ones per repository (~/.cache/st-test/<hash of the git common dir>/tree-N, one flock each, so no two runs share one; waiters are served in the build host's
cargo-priority order, then arrival), reset to the
commit and stripped of untracked and ignored files (except the megarepo members, node_modules and target/compat-baselines) before every run: the same path every time keeps cargo's fingerprints and
sccache's absolute-path keys valid, so a second run is warm. Nothing is linked in from here: when the tree
has a megarepo.lock, its members are materialized under repos/ first, with the command CI's `megarepoApplyStep` runs
(`mr apply`, from the flake's effect-utils pin), because both lanes read repos/effect-utils and their dev shells no longer
create it.

evidence-wrap, evidence-sign and evidence-verify come from the accepted base, not from the change under test.
Commands that call cargo reach the build admission shim on PATH like any other cargo; nothing here knows about
admission. With --push the signed notes are pushed to REMOTE after they verify, with the identity git uses for REMOTE
(or EVIDENCE_PUSH_TOKEN if set), as refs/tags/evidence/<tree>/<record> and refs/notes/evidence/<tree>/<record> (see
evidence-wrap); whether that identity may create them is a repository rule that has not been confirmed for agent seats.
On a host that runs the evidence log (scripts/evidence-log.service installed as a systemd user unit), a run with --push then
asks it to pull at once, without waiting: a pass counts only once the log lists it, so this saves up to one pull interval.
ST_EVIDENCE_LOG_NUDGE (a JSON argv) replaces that command; asking never changes the result.

With --out DIR every selected, relevant check is measured again, including already-covered crates, and evidence-wrap writes
DIR/<record>.pack and DIR/<record>.json instead of importing notes or pushing. DIR is resolved from the caller's working directory
before entering a clean worktree. --out and --push are incompatible; --seed --out exports without its usual implicit push.
Success means the checks passed and their packs were exported, not that the verifier counts them as covered: publication and
verification of the exported records are the publisher's responsibility. Lane-edit refusals and irrelevant checks still do not sign.

It runs `nix develop` and `pnpm install` from the tree under test, on this machine, as this user, with the host's
signing key within reach: code in that tree runs here. That is the cooperative trust of docs/st3/gate-evidence.md
("Host keys"); do not run it on a branch you would not run by hand.

Each check's --time-limit counts only the time it runs: the waits for a host build slot that scripts/ci-local reports as they happen
(CI_LOCAL_QUEUE_LOG) extend it, up to --queue-limit seconds of waiting in all, so a busy host's queue cannot use up a check's time. A
scripts/ci-local check's time counts only from ci-local's own start (its `ci-local: stage logs in` line, after its load gate): before it,
the build admission of its `nix develop` and the dev shell are waiting, which no queue log records, and only SILENCE_LIMIT seconds without
any output stop it. The note records that wait as the input pre_start_s. The summary row says how long a check waited, and for what.

Every run that gets as far as its summary appends one JSON line (version 1: start, end and total seconds, host, tree, the checks, every
summary row and, when scripts/ci-local printed its stage table, linux-gate's stages) to ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/st-test/runs.jsonl,
or to the path in ST_TEST_RUNS (`off` records nothing); scripts/fractal-test-report reads it against the per-PR suite's time objective
(docs/st3/gate-evidence.md). Recording never changes the result: a file that cannot be written is one line on stderr.

Exit 0 when every selected check passed and was signed (with --out, exported but not verified); 1 when a check failed, timed out or
could not be signed; 2 when it could not start (a refusal: dirty tree, bad argument); 3 from --status when the tree is not covered. A tree that edits
a check's lane is signed like any other (the note carries the edited lane's digest). The run exits 4 when nothing failed but a check was not signed: only
against a base whose evidence-wrap still refuses a lane edit (before 2026-10-26) is that check left "not signed" for CI, or rust-tests left a crate unsigned
because one of its tests passed only on a retry (CI's own per-crate notes refuse such a crate too, so CI runs it; linux-gate still signs and records them
as flaky_tests).
"""
import argparse
import atexit
import base64
import calendar
import codecs
import concurrent.futures
import contextlib
import errno
import fcntl
import fnmatch
import hashlib
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import inspect
import json
import os
from pathlib import Path
import re
import shlex
import select
import shutil
import signal
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import threading
import time
import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET

sys.dont_write_bytecode = True

# The local command of each check: exactly the argv the wrapper records and a policy's `wrapper` kind pins.
# A check without an entry cannot be signed from a checkout; it is not listed, and nothing pretends otherwise.
# In the order they run: typescript-client first, because it is signed for nearly every tree (genie-freshness is refused for every
# tree that edits its lane, and a cold genie dev shell takes minutes), so the pre-push hook's wait covers it first.
LOCAL = {
    'typescript-client': ['nix', 'develop', '.#web', '-c', 'bash', '-c',
                          'pnpm install --frozen-lockfile && bash scripts/ci-typescript-client'],
    'genie-freshness': ['nix', 'develop', '.#genie', '-c', 'bash', 'scripts/ci-genie-freshness'],
    'ci-script-tests': ['nix', 'develop', '.#genie', '-c', 'bash', 'scripts/ci-script-tests'],
    # The stages a linux-gate skip covers (the tests, clippy and fleet compatibility), in one run, as their CI jobs run them but
    # with a home of their own (scripts/ci-local). The VM tests and the mail redelivery canaries run daily (daily-long.yml).
    'linux-gate': ['nix', 'develop', '-c', 'bash', 'scripts/ci-local', 'tests', 'clippy', 'fleet-compat'],
    # The web lane as ONE shard that covers every kit play and app test (scripts/ci-local-web); it runs only when a web input changed.
    'fractal-web': ['nix', 'develop', '.#web', '-c', 'bash', 'scripts/ci-local-web'],
    # One signature per workspace crate (docs/per-crate-test-signing.md): the recorded command is the small script that reads one crate's
    # result out of the run's JUnit report; the run itself is RUN_TESTS, for the crates that have no passing note for their key.
    'rust-tests': ['python3', 'scripts/ci-rust-test-result'],
}
RUST_TESTS = 'rust-tests'
RUST_TESTS_COMMAND = LOCAL[RUST_TESTS]
# The tests stage, run as CI runs it with the crates to test in CI_TEST_PACKAGES and the report in CI_TEST_JUNIT.
RUN_TESTS = ['nix', 'develop', '-c', 'bash', 'scripts/ci-local', 'tests']
# nextest: 0 every test passed, 100 some test failed (the report is complete either way), 4 the selection matched no test.
RUN_COMPLETED = frozenset({0, 4, 100})
# The signer refuses an input longer than 500 characters (scripts/evidence-sign); a full run's filterset is longer than that.
FILTERSET_LIMIT = 400
# Checks that build and test the Rust workspace. Every check runs by default; these are the ones the host's capacity guard governs
# (a builds.slice scope, the kept target below), which nobody has to ask for.
BUILDS = frozenset({'linux-gate', 'fractal-web', RUST_TESTS})
# rust-tests runs by default, after linux-gate, and signs the crates that have no note from linux-gate's own nextest run (its JUnit report:
# the same workspace build and selection), so the tests are not run twice. When the gate skips linux-gate's tests stage on the per-crate
# notes, linux-gate's local command shrinks to clippy and fleet-compat and rust-tests runs the crates without a note itself
# (docs/per-crate-test-signing.md, step 5).
OPT_IN = frozenset()
# Checks whose result depends on the base as well as the tree (clippy's ratchet): the base commit is recorded as an input.
BASE_BOUND = frozenset({'linux-gate'})  # rust-tests is not: a test's result does not depend on the base
# What the fractal-web note records about the run, as CI's aggregate note does (scripts/ci-evidence-signing): one shard, every kit
# play and app test, for a pull request. The verifier's shard-count and input-order rules read these.
WEB = 'fractal-web'
WEB_INPUTS = {'kit_shard_count': '1', 'shard_results': '0=success', 'kit_inputs': 'true', 'kit_play_set': 'all',
              'app_test_set': 'all', 'event_name': 'pull_request'}
BASE_SCRIPTS = ('evidence-wrap', 'evidence-sign', 'evidence-verify')
# The checks that build use one cargo target per pool tree (<pool>/target-N, outside the tree so the cleaning keeps it), kept between
# runs, so a run does not start cold: the tree's path is the same every run, so the workspace's own crates rebuild only when they
# changed and the dependencies and build scripts are reused. It is removed before a run once it passes TARGET_MAX_GB (the run holds
# the tree's lock, so nobody else uses it). ST_EVIDENCE_TARGET=PATH names one shared target for all trees instead (kept as before:
# trimmed only while no other run uses it); ST_EVIDENCE_TARGET=off builds in the tree itself; a CARGO_TARGET_DIR the caller set is
# left alone.
# One run (test build + clippy + fleet-compat profiles) ends near 60 GB; with incremental compilation on, a quarter to a half of that is debug/incremental
# (a build host, 2026-10-23: target-0 99 GB of which incremental 40, deps 55, examples 5). The check is only made before a run, so a limit below one run's
# size would empty the target every time and nothing would ever be warm: 80 GB lets one run's output stay.
TARGET_MAX_GB = 80.0
# The pool of persistent worktrees: ST_TEST_TREES trees (default: the host's heavy slots from ~/.config/cargo-heavy-slots, plus one)
# under ST_TEST_POOL (default ~/.cache/st-test). ST_TEST_TREES=off runs in a fresh throwaway worktree, as before.
POOL_WAIT_SECONDS = 1  # how often a waiter looks at the tree queue again
# Ignored inputs a pool tree keeps between runs (git clean -e): the megarepo members, the installed dependencies and the downloaded
# compat baselines cost minutes to materialize, install and download again. They are not tracked files, so the tree stays exact.
POOL_KEEP = ('/repos/', 'node_modules/', '/target/compat-baselines/')
POOL_SETTINGS = {}  # the ST_TEST_* settings (pool, pool cap, signing jobs, member limit, runs file) as the caller set them: read before the seat scrub drops every ST_* name
SETTING_NAMES = ('ST_TEST_TREES', 'ST_TEST_POOL', 'ST_TEST_POOL_MAX_GB', 'ST_TEST_SIGN_JOBS', 'ST_TEST_MEMBERS_LIMIT', 'ST_TEST_RUNS',
                 'ST_TEST_MAX_LOAD', 'ST_TEST_LOAD_WAIT_MINUTES')
POOL_MAX_GB = 60.0  # all repositories' pools together under the pool directory; ST_TEST_POOL_MAX_GB (0 or off: no cap) replaces it


# A host-local load limit (set by whoever runs the host, never in the repo): the 1-minute load average above which a run does not start its
# heavy work. The limit is a number in ~/.config/st-test/max-load (or ST_TEST_MAX_LOAD); no file and no variable means no limit. While the load is
# above it the run WAITS and says why every minute, up to ST_TEST_LOAD_WAIT_MINUTES (default 30), then fails with that reason: never silently.
LOAD_WAIT_MINUTES = 30
LOAD_ANNOUNCE_SECONDS = 60


def load_limit():
    value = POOL_SETTINGS.get('ST_TEST_MAX_LOAD')
    if value is None:
        try:
            value = (Path.home() / '.config/st-test/max-load').read_text().split()[0]
        except (OSError, IndexError):
            return None
    try:
        limit = float(value)
    except ValueError:
        raise Failure(f'the host load limit {value!r} must be a number (a load average)')
    return limit if limit > 0 else None


def wait_for_load(limit, read_load=lambda: os.getloadavg()[0], sleep=time.sleep, clock=time.monotonic, deadline_minutes=None):
    """Return once the 1-minute load average is at or below `limit`; say why we wait every minute; fail after the deadline."""
    if deadline_minutes is None:
        value = POOL_SETTINGS.get('ST_TEST_LOAD_WAIT_MINUTES')
        try:
            deadline_minutes = float(value) if value is not None else LOAD_WAIT_MINUTES
        except ValueError:
            raise Failure(f'ST_TEST_LOAD_WAIT_MINUTES={value!r} must be a number of minutes')
    started, announced = clock(), None
    while True:
        load = read_load()
        if load <= limit:
            return
        now = clock()
        if now - started >= deadline_minutes * 60:
            raise Failure(f'the host stayed above its load limit: load1 {load:.0f} > {limit:g} (host limit) for {deadline_minutes:g} min; nothing was started')
        if announced is None or now - announced >= LOAD_ANNOUNCE_SECONDS:
            print(f'fractal-test: waiting: load1 {load:.0f} > {limit:g} (host limit)', file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
            announced = now
        sleep(min(10, LOAD_ANNOUNCE_SECONDS))


class Failure(Exception):
    pass


SELECTING = ('GIT_DIR', 'GIT_WORK_TREE', 'GIT_INDEX_FILE', 'GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY', 'GIT_ALTERNATE_OBJECT_DIRECTORIES', 'GIT_COMMON_DIR',
                  'GIT_NAMESPACE', 'GIT_PREFIX', 'GIT_QUARANTINE_PATH', 'GIT_REPLACE_REF_BASE', 'GIT_NO_REPLACE_OBJECTS', 'GIT_GRAFT_FILE',
                  'GIT_SHALLOW_FILE', 'GIT_INTERNAL_SUPER_PREFIX', 'GIT_AUTHOR_NAME', 'GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL', 'GIT_AUTHOR_DATE',
                  'GIT_COMMITTER_NAME', 'GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL', 'GIT_COMMITTER_DATE')


def selecting(name):
    """A variable that names a repository or an identity: git exports them to a hook, and a check that kept them would act on the
    caller's repository. Transport and credential variables (GIT_SSH_COMMAND, GIT_ASKPASS, GIT_CONFIG_*, ...) are not here."""
    return name in SELECTING or name.startswith('GIT_PUSH_OPTION')


SEAT_PREFIXES = ('ST_', 'ST2_', 'ST3_', 'FRACTAL_', 'CLAUDE', 'ANTHROPIC_', 'PTY_')
KEPT_SEAT = ('ST_AGENT', 'ST_SKIP_EVIDENCE')  # exact names, plus every ST_EVIDENCE* switch; this list is the only exception mechanism


# The directories every check's PATH is made of, in this order (those that exist), plus the one `nix` is in: cargo's build scripts record
# PATH, so two seats whose PATHs differ (their own tools, state directories, dev shells) would rebuild nearly the whole workspace in one
# pool tree. The perf dev shell fixes its PATH the same way. ~/.cargo/bin stays in it: `cargo clippy -V` prints `clippy 0.1.97` with it and
# `clippy 0.1.97 (2d8144b788 2026-07-07)` without, and the clippy ratchet's baseline holds the former (measured on a build host, 2026-10-23).
# ST_EVIDENCE_PATH=keep leaves the caller's PATH alone.
CHECK_PATH = ('~/.local/bin', '~/.cargo/bin', '/run/current-system/sw/bin', '/nix/var/nix/profiles/default/bin', '/usr/bin', '/bin')


def check_path():
    entries = [os.path.expanduser(entry) for entry in CHECK_PATH]
    nix = shutil.which('nix')
    if nix:
        entries.append(os.path.dirname(nix))
    kept = []
    for entry in entries:
        if os.path.isdir(entry) and entry not in kept:
            kept.append(entry)
    return ':'.join(kept)


def scrub_seat_environment():
    """A CI runner has none of the variables an agent seat carries (identity, mission, session, daemon endpoint, hook and messaging
    settings, and the pty session it runs in), and the checks must see what CI sees: tests that read them fail or behave differently
    under a seat (the ci-local-gate drill's claude hook tests did). A seat's PTY_ROOT names its live pty root: the legacy-runner tests
    that spawn sessions then all spawn there instead of under their own catalogs, and concurrent ones collide (stream_authoring_cli's
    "spawning pty 'example-linux.worker' failed"). FRACTAL_* are the seat's own names for the same things (FRACTAL_ENDPOINT,
    FRACTAL_AGENT): a check that kept them could reach the live daemon as the seat. Every check starts in `nix develop`, whose shell
    sets the dev shell's FRACTAL_* settings (flake.nix) itself, and scripts/ci-local drops the rest for its stages. ST_AGENT stays because the host ranks `nix develop` starts by it (so a check can still read it: the nix wrapper that needs it
    starts the check); the evidence switches stay. The kept list is the only exception mechanism for a future ST_* switch."""
    for name in [name for name in os.environ
                 if name.startswith(SEAT_PREFIXES) and name not in KEPT_SEAT and not name.startswith('ST_EVIDENCE')]:
        del os.environ[name]


def scrub_git_environment():
    """Drop the repository- and identity-selecting GIT_* variables (st2#3014); the notes push keeps its transport variables."""
    for name in [name for name in os.environ if selecting(name)]:
        del os.environ[name]


def git(*args, cwd=None, env=None, check=True, stdin=None):
    done = subprocess.run(['git', *args], cwd=cwd, capture_output=True, input=stdin,
                          env=dict(os.environ, **(env or {})))
    if check and done.returncode != 0:
        raise Failure(f'git {args[0]} failed: {done.stderr.decode(errors="replace").strip()[-400:]}')
    return done


def text(done):
    return done.stdout.decode().strip()


TARGET = []  # [commit] when --seed names the tree to sign instead of HEAD


GATE_TREE = {}  # (head, base commit) -> (commit, why): the commit whose tree the CI gate will judge, and the reason when it is not HEAD


def head_commit():
    return TARGET[0] if TARGET else text(git('rev-parse', 'HEAD'))


def load_base_gate(base_commit):
    """scripts/ci-evidence-gate as it is at the accepted base, as a module (None when the base has none): the very code the CI gate runs."""
    shown = git('show', f'{base_commit}:scripts/ci-evidence-gate', check=False)
    if shown.returncode != 0:
        return None
    path = Path(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix='st-test-gate-')) / 'ci-evidence-gate'
    path.write_bytes(shown.stdout)
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader('ci_evidence_gate_at_base', str(path))
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader(loader.name, loader)
    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    try:
        loader.exec_module(module)
    except Exception:
        return None
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(path.parent, ignore_errors=True)
    return module


def gate_commit(base):
    """(commit, why): the commit whose tree the CI gate will judge for the branch being pushed.

    Normally the head itself (docs/st3/gate-evidence.md, "Which tree a pull request run asks about"): the tree an agent built and signed
    is the tree that counts, whether or not main has moved since. But when protected files moved on main since the branch point (or the
    branch is far behind), the gate falls back to the MERGE tree, which nobody signed: so a signature on the head voids. The rule is the
    gate's own, taken from the base (`branch_point_verdict`, the function choose_tree calls). In that case this makes the merge of the
    head with the base (git merge-tree: nothing is checked out here) as a commit, and the run signs and checks that. `why` is empty
    when it is the head."""
    head = head_commit()
    if TARGET:  # --seed names the tree to sign
        return head, ''
    base_commit = text(git('rev-parse', '--verify', '-q', base + '^{commit}', check=False))
    if not base_commit:
        return head, ''
    key = (head, base_commit)
    if key in GATE_TREE:
        return GATE_TREE[key]
    result = (head, '')
    reason = fallback_reason(head, base_commit)
    if reason:
        merged = git('merge-tree', '--write-tree', '--no-messages', head, base_commit, check=False)
        if merged.returncode != 0:
            raise Failure(f'CI will judge the merge of this branch with {base} ({reason}), and it does not merge cleanly: merge {base} into the branch first')
        tree = merged.stdout.decode().split()[0]
        env = {'GIT_AUTHOR_NAME': 'st-test', 'GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL': 'st-test@example.invalid', 'GIT_COMMITTER_NAME': 'st-test',
               'GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL': 'st-test@example.invalid'}
        commit = text(git('commit-tree', tree, '-p', head, '-p', base_commit, '-m', f'merge of {head[:12]} with {base}', env=env))
        result = (commit, f'{reason}: CI will judge the merge of this branch with {base}, so that is what is signed')
    GATE_TREE[key] = result
    return result


CHECKS = ()  # the checks this run signs (main sets it): the gate decides per check whether main's protected changes are that check's


def fallback_reason(head, base_commit):
    """Why the CI gate would judge the merge of `head` with the base rather than the head's own tree for some check this run signs (the
    base's `branch_point_verdict` Fallback: the branch is more than HEAD_TREE_MAX_BEHIND commits behind, or main moved protected files
    since the branch point that are a check's CI inputs), or None when it keeps the head tree for all of them: also when the base is in
    the head already (the merge is the head) or the base has no such rule. A base gate that predates per-check CI inputs is asked once,
    without a check, as before."""
    if git('merge-base', '--is-ancestor', base_commit, head, check=False).returncode == 0:
        return None
    verdict = getattr(load_base_gate(base_commit), 'branch_point_verdict', None)
    if not verdict:
        return None
    branch_point = text(git('merge-base', base_commit, head))
    per_check = 'check' in inspect.signature(verdict).parameters
    for check in (CHECKS if per_check and CHECKS else (None,)):
        try:
            if check is None:
                verdict(branch_point, head, base_commit, base_commit)
            else:
                verdict(branch_point, head, base_commit, base_commit, None, check)
        except Exception as reason:  # the gate's Fallback, or anything its rule raises: it asks about the merge tree
            return str(reason) or type(reason).__name__
    return None


def merge_base_in(base):
    """When the CI gate would judge the merge tree (fallback_reason), merge the base into the branch in this checkout first, as a new head
    commit, and return it (None when the head is signed as it is). The tree then signed is the head's own, which stays the tree CI judges
    for HEAD_TREE_MAX_BEHIND more commits of main; a signed merge tree is void at main's next commit, and a run that waited hours for a
    pool tree past the bound (or after main moved a protected file) was lost to whatever landed during it. Runs after the wait, at the
    base's tip as just fetched. Stops (Failure) on a conflict, with the branch as it was, or when the checkout gained uncommitted changes
    to tracked files during the wait. Not for --seed, which names its tree."""
    if TARGET:
        return None
    head = head_commit()
    base_commit = text(git('rev-parse', '--verify', '-q', base + '^{commit}', check=False))
    reason = fallback_reason(head, base_commit) if base_commit else None
    if not reason:
        return None
    if git('status', '--porcelain', '--untracked-files=no').stdout.strip():
        raise Failure(f'{reason}, so fractal-test merges {base} into the branch before it signs, but the checkout now has uncommitted changes '
                      'to tracked files: commit them and run again')
    branch = text(git('symbolic-ref', '-q', '--short', 'HEAD', check=False))
    message = (f'Merge {base} into {branch or "HEAD"}\n\nfractal-test merged {base} ({base_commit[:12]}) before signing: {reason}, so CI would '
               'otherwise judge the merge tree, which no signature on the head covers.')
    done = git('merge', '--no-ff', '--no-edit', '-m', message, base_commit, check=False)
    if done.returncode != 0:
        conflicts = text(git('diff', '--name-only', '--diff-filter=U', check=False)).split()
        git('merge', '--abort', check=False)
        raise Failure(f'{reason}, so fractal-test merges {base} into the branch before it signs, and it does not merge cleanly'
                      f'{" (" + ", ".join(conflicts[:6]) + ")" if conflicts else " (" + (text(done) + " " + done.stderr.decode(errors="replace")).strip()[-300:] + ")"}'
                      f': merge {base} into the branch yourself, then run again')
    merged = head_commit()
    print(f'fractal-test: {reason}: merged {base} ({base_commit[:12]}) into {branch or "the detached HEAD"} as {merged}; '
          'the head tree is what is signed', flush=True)
    return merged


def push_branch(remote, base, merged):
    """Push the merge commit merge_base_in made to the branch of the same name on `remote`, never forced, as any push (the pre-push hook
    asks --status). A detached HEAD, or a branch named as the base's, is not pushed: the command is printed. A remote branch with commits
    this one lacks stops the run (Failure), and nothing is pushed."""
    branch = text(git('symbolic-ref', '-q', '--short', 'HEAD', check=False))
    if not branch or branch in (base, base.partition('/')[2]):
        print(f'fractal-test: the merge {merged[:12]} is not pushed ({"a detached HEAD" if not branch else f"{branch} is the base branch"}); push it yourself: '
              f'git push {remote} {merged}:refs/heads/<branch>', flush=True)
        return
    if git('fetch', '-q', remote, f'refs/heads/{branch}', check=False).returncode == 0:
        theirs = text(git('rev-parse', 'FETCH_HEAD'))
        if git('merge-base', '--is-ancestor', theirs, merged, check=False).returncode != 0:
            raise Failure(f'{remote}/{branch} has commits this branch lacks ({theirs[:12]}), so the merge {merged[:12]} is not pushed: '
                          f'bring them in, then run again (never force)')
    done = git('push', remote, f'{merged}:refs/heads/{branch}', check=False)
    if done.returncode != 0:
        raise Failure(f'pushing the merge {merged[:12]} to {remote}/{branch} failed: {done.stderr.decode(errors="replace").strip()[-400:]}')
    print(f'fractal-test: pushed {branch} at the merge {merged[:12]} to {remote}', flush=True)


REEXEC = 'ST_TEST_REEXEC'  # set to the base commit in the base's copy that run_base_copy started: it never re-executes again


def run_base_copy(argv):
    """Replace this process with the base's self-contained scripts/st-test, as BASE_SCRIPTS does for the wrapper, signer and verifier.
    Every seat runs fractal-test from its own branch, so without this, how runs queue for pool trees and build slots (and which commit they
    sign) stays at whatever the branch last merged, for days, and old branches keep racing while new ones queue. A branch's own runner
    changes are still tested by ci-script-tests (fractal-test-test runs from the tree), and that is what is signed. There is no opt-out.
    This copy runs only when the base's st-test is absent, empty or this file, or this already is the base's copy (ST_TEST_REEXEC).
    The human-facing fractal-test shim cannot be copied alone; retain st-test until every supported base permits contraction."""
    if os.environ.get(REEXEC):
        return
    scrub_git_environment()
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(add_help=False)
    parser.add_argument('--base', default='origin/main')
    base = parser.parse_known_args(argv)[0].base
    try:
        commit = fetch_base(base)
    except Exception:  # offline, or no such remote: the base as this clone has it, else this copy
        commit = text(git('rev-parse', '--verify', '-q', base + '^{commit}', check=False))
    if not commit:
        return
    # arn:lmig:smalltalk:2026-10-27-st-test-shim — copy the self-contained implementation, never the adjacent-file shim.
    shown = git('show', f'{commit}:scripts/st-test', check=False)
    if shown.returncode or not shown.stdout or shown.stdout == Path(__file__).read_bytes():
        return
    scratch = Path(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix='st-test-base-'))
    path = scratch / 'st-test'
    path.write_bytes(shown.stdout)
    path.chmod(0o700)
    print(f"fractal-test: running the base's st-test ({base} at {commit[:12]}), not this branch's copy", file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
    os.execve(sys.executable, [sys.executable, str(path), *argv], dict(os.environ, **{REEXEC: commit}))


FETCH_LOCK_TRIES, FETCH_LOCK_BACKOFF = 3, 2.0  # seconds before the second try, twice that before the third


def fetch_base(base):
    """The base's commit at its current tip: a remote-tracking base (origin/main) is fetched first, since main moves; the lag and the
    policy are read at that tip. A shared clone has many worktrees and seats fetching at once, and git refuses to update a remote-tracking
    ref another fetch moved under it ("cannot lock ref 'refs/remotes/origin/main': is at X but expected Y"): that fetch is tried again,
    up to FETCH_LOCK_TRIES times with a short backoff, as the race is no failure of the run's (2026-10-27: one ended a run after its
    44-minute wait for a pool tree). Any other fetch error fails at once."""
    remote, _, branch = base.partition('/')
    if branch:
        for attempt in range(1, FETCH_LOCK_TRIES + 1):
            done = git('fetch', '-q', remote, branch, check=False)
            if done.returncode == 0:
                break
            error = done.stderr.decode(errors='replace').strip()
            if 'cannot lock ref' not in error or attempt == FETCH_LOCK_TRIES:
                raise Failure(f'git fetch failed: {error[-400:]}')
            print(f'fractal-test: another fetch moved {base} at the same moment; fetching again ({attempt + 1} of {FETCH_LOCK_TRIES})',
                  file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
            time.sleep(FETCH_LOCK_BACKOFF * attempt)
    return text(git('rev-parse', '--verify', '-q', base + '^{commit}'))


def target_commit(base):
    """The commit that is signed: the one whose tree the CI gate will judge (gate_commit: the head, or the merge with the base when
    the gate falls back to it); with --seed, the commit it names."""
    return gate_commit(base)[0]


def branch_point(commit, base):
    """Where the branch left the base (the base itself when the branch holds it): the commit whose lane definition the tree is
    held to. The CI gate judges a pull request head the same way (docs/st3/gate-evidence.md, "Which lane definition a pull request
    is held to"): a branch that only lags main changed no lane file itself. When main changed the lane entry of a check this run signs
    since then, the base: the verifier holds a head to its branch point only while that check's own entry is the base's.
    """
    point = text(git('merge-base', commit, base))
    texts = [git('show', f'{at}:.st/required-lanes.json', check=False) for at in (point, base)]
    if any(done.returncode != 0 for done in texts):
        return point if all(done.returncode != 0 for done in texts) else base

    def entries(done):
        try:
            lanes = json.loads(done.stdout)['lanes']
        except (ValueError, KeyError, TypeError):
            return None
        return {name: json.dumps(lanes.get(name), sort_keys=True) for name in (CHECKS or lanes)}
    here, there = (entries(done) for done in texts)
    return point if here is not None and here == there else base


def size_gb(path):
    total = 0
    for root, _, files in os.walk(path):
        for name in files:
            try:
                total += os.lstat(os.path.join(root, name)).st_blocks * 512
            except OSError:
                pass
    return total / 1e9


def trim(target):
    """Empty a target past TARGET_MAX_GB. Only a directory cargo made (it writes CACHEDIR.TAG into a target) is emptied: never one a
    caller named for something else."""
    if (target / 'CACHEDIR.TAG').is_file() and size_gb(target) > TARGET_MAX_GB:
        shutil.rmtree(target, ignore_errors=True)
        target.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)


def shared_target(tree_target=None):
    """The kept target for the checks that build and a lock held while they use it, or (None, None) when it is off or the caller set one.

    With a pool tree's own target (and no ST_EVIDENCE_TARGET), that target: the tree's lock is held, so there is nothing to lock here."""
    if os.environ.get('ST_EVIDENCE_TARGET') == 'off' or os.environ.get('CARGO_TARGET_DIR'):
        return None, None
    if tree_target and not os.environ.get('ST_EVIDENCE_TARGET'):
        tree_target.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        trim(tree_target)
        return tree_target, None
    target = Path(os.environ.get('ST_EVIDENCE_TARGET') or Path.home() / '.cache/st-test-target')
    target.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    lock = open(target.parent / f'{target.name}.lock', 'w')
    # A target several trees share is held EXCLUSIVELY for the whole run, builds and tests: cargo's own lock orders builds, but not
    # one run's builds against another run's tests, and two runs on different trees sharing one target once relinked st3 under each
    # other's tests (a test ran the other tree's st3), so a note could name a tree whose binaries were not the ones tested. A second
    # run waits for the first, and says so.
    try:
        fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
    except BlockingIOError:
        print(f'fractal-test: another run is using {target}; waiting for it (one run builds and tests in it at a time)', file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
        fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
    trim(target)  # nobody else is using it: the moment to keep it small
    return target, lock


def governed(argv, name):
    """`argv` (evidence-wrap and the check under it) as the host's build cap governs a check that builds: a builds.slice scope around
    everything it starts, so compiles a test runner starts without the shim (cargo-nextest, sccache) still get the slice's CPU, IO and
    memory limits, while each cargo command inside takes the host's build slots through the admission shim as any build does.

    No heavy-slot ticket is held for the whole check (`cargo --stage-slot`): the checks' tests run under the test guardian
    (scripts/fractal_test_process.py), which detaches from its parents, and the shim lets a cargo command skip the queue under a held
    stage only when the stage is its ancestor. The cargo commands under the guardian then queued for the one heavy slot their own
    stage held, until the stage limit killed it (2026-10-23, on a build host). The recorded command is unchanged: only what starts
    evidence-wrap changes. A host without a user systemd bus runs it as before, and says so; ST_EVIDENCE_GOVERN=off does too."""
    if os.environ.get('ST_EVIDENCE_GOVERN') == 'off':
        return argv
    runtime = os.environ.get('XDG_RUNTIME_DIR')
    scope = shutil.which('systemd-run')  # by its full path: the check's PATH is a fixed one, not the caller's
    if scope and runtime and Path(runtime, 'bus').exists():
        return [scope, '--user', '--scope', '--quiet', '--collect', '--slice=builds.slice', '--'] + argv
    print(f'fractal-test: {name} runs without the host build cap (no user systemd bus for a builds.slice scope)', file=sys.stderr)
    return argv


def web_changes(base):
    """(relevant paths, kit paths) of HEAD since its merge base with the accepted base, by the base's own scripts/ci-fractal-web-changes
    (`relevant_paths` and `kit_paths`, the functions its CI job runs); None when that cannot be told."""
    source = git('show', f'{base}:scripts/ci-fractal-web-changes', check=False)
    start = git('merge-base', base, 'HEAD', check=False)
    if source.returncode or start.returncode:
        return None
    diff = git('diff', '--name-only', '--no-renames', '-z', text(start), 'HEAD', check=False)
    if diff.returncode:
        return None
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(prefix='st-test-') as scratch:
        path = Path(scratch) / 'ci_fractal_web_changes.py'
        path.write_bytes(source.stdout)
        try:
            loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader('ci_fractal_web_changes_for_st_test', str(path))
            module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(importlib.util.spec_from_loader(loader.name, loader))
            loader.exec_module(module)
            changed = [name for name in diff.stdout.decode().split('\0') if name]
            return list(module.relevant_paths(changed)), list(module.kit_paths(changed)), text(start)
        except Exception:
            return None


def web_unchanged(base):
    """Why CI would not run the fractal-web lane for HEAD: no web-relevant path changed since HEAD's merge base with the accepted base,
    by the base's own scripts/ci-fractal-web-changes rules. None when one did, or when that cannot be told (then the lane runs)."""
    changes = web_changes(base)
    if changes is None:
        return None
    relevant, _, start = changes
    return None if relevant else f'no web input changed since {start[:12]}: CI does not run the lane'


def run_input_arguments(check, base):
    """The `--run-input KEY=VALUE` arguments the CI gate passes the verifier for `check` (ci-evidence-gate RUN_INPUTS): fractal-web's kit_inputs,
    which its change detection (scripts/ci-fractal-web-changes) answers for this diff; true when that cannot be told, as the job runs all lanes
    then. A verifier whose base policy requires such an input never counts a run that does not say it as covered ('no_run_input')."""
    if check != WEB:
        return []
    changes = web_changes(base)
    kit = True if changes is None else bool(changes[1])
    return ['--run-input', f"kit_inputs={'true' if kit else 'false'}"]


def edited_lanes_unsigned(base):
    """True when the base's evidence-wrap refuses a tree that edits its lane (it lacks SIGNS_LANE_EDITS): only then is a lane edit left to CI."""
    done = git('show', f'{base}:scripts/evidence-wrap', check=False)
    return done.returncode != 0 or b'\nSIGNS_LANE_EDITS = True\n' not in done.stdout


def lane_names(base):
    """The lanes the accepted base defines in .st/required-lanes.json (none when the file is absent or unreadable: then nothing changes)."""
    done = git('show', f'{base}:.st/required-lanes.json', check=False)
    if done.returncode != 0:
        return set()
    try:
        lanes = json.loads(done.stdout.decode())['lanes']
        return set(lanes) if isinstance(lanes, dict) else set()
    except (ValueError, KeyError, UnicodeDecodeError, TypeError):
        return set()


def policy_checks(base):
    """The checks the accepted base's skip policy names (none when it is absent or unreadable)."""
    done = git('show', f'{base}:.st/evidence-skip-policy.json', check=False)
    try:
        return list(json.loads(done.stdout.decode())['checks']) if done.returncode == 0 else []
    except (ValueError, KeyError, UnicodeDecodeError, TypeError):
        return []


def lane_edits(verifier_path, directory, base, check, tree):
    """The paths of `check`'s lane definition that `tree` changes relative to `base`, the branch point (empty when it changes none). evidence-wrap refuses
    to sign such a tree; asking first lets the run say so plainly, as "not signed", instead of reporting a failed check."""
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader('evidence_verify_for_st_test', verifier_path)
    verifier = importlib.util.module_from_spec(importlib.util.spec_from_loader(loader.name, loader))
    loader.exec_module(verifier)
    try:
        rules = verifier.lane_rules(git('show', f'{base}:{verifier.LANES_PATH}').stdout.decode(), check)
        base_tree = text(git('rev-parse', '--verify', '--end-of-options', base + '^{tree}'))
        return verifier.lane_definition(str(directory), base_tree, tree, rules)['changed']
    except verifier.Failure:
        return []  # evidence-wrap gives the reason


def behind(base):
    """How many commits of the base this branch lacks (0 when it holds the base's tip)."""
    done = git('rev-list', '--count', f'{head_commit()}..{base}', check=False)
    return int(text(done)) if done.returncode == 0 and text(done).isdigit() else None


def target_worktree(base, scratch):
    """(directory, commit): a clean throwaway worktree of that commit.

    Always a fresh worktree, never this checkout, so untracked or ignored files here cannot change the result, and nothing
    is linked in from here (materialize_members fills repos/ from the tree's own locks)."""
    commit = target_commit(base)
    directory = Path(scratch) / 'tree'
    git('worktree', 'add', '--detach', '-q', str(directory), commit)
    return directory, commit


def needs_tree(checks):
    """Whether a run of `checks` takes a pool tree: what a tree keeps is a warm cargo target, which only a check that builds (BUILDS) uses. A run
    of light checks alone (ci-script-tests, typescript-client, genie-freshness) takes a throwaway worktree instead of waiting behind runs that
    build for a tree it does not need (2026-10-26: a 10-second ci-script-tests rerun waited behind 30-minute runs)."""
    return any(name in BUILDS for name in checks)


def heavy_slots():
    """The host's heavy build slots, by the cargo shim's own formula, so every box counts its trees as it counts its slots: CARGO_HEAVY_SLOTS,
    else what ~/.config/cargo-heavy-slots holds (the host's guard rewrites it), else 1; anything that is not a number is 1."""
    value = os.environ.get('CARGO_HEAVY_SLOTS', '')
    if not value:
        try:
            value = (Path.home() / '.config/cargo-heavy-slots').read_text().strip()
        except OSError:
            value = '1'
    return int(value) if value.isdigit() else 1


def pool_size():
    """How many persistent trees the pool holds: ST_TEST_TREES, else the host's heavy slots plus one; None (off) for fresh worktrees."""
    value = POOL_SETTINGS.get('ST_TEST_TREES', '')
    if value == 'off':
        return None
    if value:
        if not value.isdigit() or int(value) < 1:
            raise Failure(f'ST_TEST_TREES={value!r} must be a positive number or "off"')
        return int(value)
    return max(heavy_slots(), 1) + 1


# Slot first (2026-10-28, Factory's P0 from the st-test SLO report: on one build host the slowest run waited 42,265 s for one of its trees,
# while the linux-gate checks of the runs holding them waited 1,000-2,400 s at a time for a build slot). A tree held while its run waits for a
# build slot is a tree nobody can use, and a tree cannot be given back while a check is at work in it, so the best ticket takes a free tree
# only when a build started now would get a heavy slot at once. TREE_SLOT_WAIT_LIMIT bounds that wait, as the admission gate bounds its own
# (MAX_WAIT_SECONDS): past it the tree is taken anyway, so a host whose slots never come free starves no run; it then waits in the tree, as before.
TREE_SLOT_WAIT_LIMIT = 2700


def heavy_tickets_waiting(queue):
    """How many live heavy tickets wait in the cargo shim's queue directory `queue` (CLASS.RANK.NANOSECONDS.PID). Read only: the files
    are the shim's, so a dead one is skipped, never removed."""
    count = 0
    with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
        for name in os.listdir(queue):
            parts = name.split('.')
            if len(parts) != 4 or parts[0] != 'heavy' or not all(part.isdigit() for part in parts[1:]):
                continue
            nanos, pid = int(parts[2]), int(parts[3])
            try:
                os.kill(pid, 0)
            except ProcessLookupError:
                continue
            except PermissionError:
                pass
            started = process_started_ns(pid)
            if started is not None and started > nanos + 2 * 10**9:  # the pid now names a younger process: that waiter is gone
                continue
            count += 1
    return count


def build_slot_ready():
    """(ready, why): whether a heavy build started now would get one of the host's heavy slots at once. The cargo shim's slots are flocks on
    $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/cargo-slots/heavyN: a free one is taken and let go at once to see it, and the heavy tickets already waiting there are
    served first, so this run is ready only while more slots are free than tickets wait, and while the build admission gate (`build-admission
    check heavy`, as the shim asks it) would admit a heavy build now. A host without the shim's slots, or a gate that cannot be read, is ready."""
    slots = Path(os.environ.get('XDG_RUNTIME_DIR') or '/tmp') / 'cargo-slots'
    if not slots.is_dir():
        return True, 'this host has no build slots'
    total, free = heavy_slots(), 0
    for j in range(total):
        try:
            with open(slots / f'heavy{j}', 'a') as probe:
                fcntl.flock(probe, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)  # let go when the file is closed, before the shim's next look
                free += 1
        except BlockingIOError:
            continue
        except OSError:
            return True, 'the build slots cannot be read'
    waiting = heavy_tickets_waiting(slots / 'queue')
    if free <= waiting:
        return False, f'{total - free} of {total} heavy slots busy, {waiting} heavy build(s) waiting for one'
    gate = os.environ.get('BUILD_ADMISSION_BIN') or str(Path.home() / '.local/bin/build-admission')
    if os.environ.get('BUILD_ADMISSION') != 'off' and os.access(gate, os.X_OK):
        try:
            code = subprocess.run([gate, 'check', 'heavy', 'st-test:tree'], stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                                  stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=30).returncode
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
            code = 2
        if code == 1:
            return False, 'the build admission gate would not admit a heavy build now (load or memory)'
    return True, f'{free} of {total} heavy slots free'


def normalise_origin(url):
    """A remote URL as the same repository whatever way it was cloned: https://host/o/r.git, ssh://git@host/o/r and git@host:o/r.git alike."""
    url = url.strip()
    match = re.fullmatch(r'(?:[\w.-]+@)?([\w.-]+):(?!//)(.+)', url)  # scp-like: git@host:owner/repo.git
    if match and not url.startswith(('/', '.')):
        host, path = match.groups()
    else:
        match = re.fullmatch(r'[a-z][a-z0-9+.-]*://(?:[^/@]+@)?([^/:]+)(?::\d+)?/(.+)', url, re.I)
        host, path = match.groups() if match else ('', url)
    path = re.sub(r'(\.git)?/*$', '', path.strip('/') if host else path.rstrip('/'))
    return f'{host}/{path}'.lower() if host else path.lower()


def pool_root():
    """The pool of this repository's ORIGIN: every clone of one origin shares it (one warm target and tree path per slot), so a throwaway
    clone reuses the warm slots of an existing one instead of building a 35-44 GB target of its own. A clone without an origin keeps a pool
    of its own, keyed by its git common dir."""
    origin = text(git('config', '--get', 'remote.origin.url', check=False))
    if origin:
        key = 'o-' + hashlib.sha256(normalise_origin(origin).encode()).hexdigest()[:16]
    else:
        common = text(git('rev-parse', '--path-format=absolute', '--git-common-dir'))
        key = 'c-' + hashlib.sha256(os.path.realpath(common).encode()).hexdigest()[:16]
    return pool_base() / 'pools' / key


def pool_base():
    return Path(POOL_SETTINGS.get('ST_TEST_POOL') or Path.home() / '.cache/st-test')


def pool_max_gb():
    value = POOL_SETTINGS.get('ST_TEST_POOL_MAX_GB', '')
    if value in ('off', '0'):
        return None
    if not value:
        return POOL_MAX_GB
    try:
        limit = float(value)
    except ValueError:
        raise Failure(f'ST_TEST_POOL_MAX_GB={value!r} must be a number of GB, 0 or "off"')
    if limit < 0:
        raise Failure(f'ST_TEST_POOL_MAX_GB={value!r} must not be negative')
    return limit or None


def du_gb(path):
    """The disk a directory holds, in GB (du, which is far quicker than walking a 60 GB build tree from here)."""
    done = subprocess.run(['du', '-sk', '--', str(path)], capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    try:
        return int(done.stdout.split()[0]) * 1024 / 1e9
    except (IndexError, ValueError):
        return size_gb(path)


def pool_units(base):
    """[(last used, directory of the unit's lock, N, [paths])]: every pool tree with its target, across all the repositories' pools."""
    units = {}
    pools = sorted(path for path in base.iterdir() if path.is_dir() and path.name != 'pools') if base.is_dir() else []  # the old layout: one per clone
    if (base / 'pools').is_dir():
        pools += sorted(path for path in (base / 'pools').iterdir() if path.is_dir())
    for repo in pools:
        for entry in repo.iterdir():
            match = re.fullmatch(r'(tree|target)-(\d+)(\.lock)?', entry.name)
            if match:
                units.setdefault((repo, int(match.group(2))), []).append(entry)
    found = []
    for (repo, n), paths in units.items():
        lock = repo / f'tree-{n}.lock'
        try:
            used = lock.stat().st_mtime
        except OSError:
            used = min((path.stat().st_mtime for path in paths if path.exists()), default=0)
        found.append((used, repo, n, [path for path in paths if path.name != lock.name]))
    return sorted(found)


def evict_to_cap(base, limit_gb, keep=None):
    """Keep the pool directory under `limit_gb` GB in all: before a run, remove the least recently used trees and their targets whose lock
    is free, oldest first, until it fits. A unit someone holds is never touched (`keep` is the one this run holds). Says what it removed."""
    units = pool_units(base)
    sizes = {(repo, n): sum(du_gb(path) for path in paths) for _, repo, n, paths in units}
    total = sum(sizes.values())
    for used, repo, n, paths in units:
        if total <= limit_gb:
            break
        if (repo, n) == keep:
            continue
        try:
            lock = open(repo / f'tree-{n}.lock', 'a')
        except OSError:
            continue
        try:
            fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
        except BlockingIOError:
            lock.close()
            continue
        try:
            for path in paths:
                subprocess.run(['chmod', '-R', 'u+rwX', '--', str(path)], check=False, capture_output=True)
                shutil.rmtree(path, ignore_errors=True)
            total -= sizes[(repo, n)]
            idle = max(0, int((time.time() - used) / 60))
            print(f'fractal-test: pool over {limit_gb:g} GB: evicted {repo.name}/tree-{n} and target-{n} ({sizes[(repo, n)]:.1f} GB, idle {idle} min)', file=sys.stderr)
        finally:
            lock.close()
    if total > limit_gb:
        print(f'fractal-test: pool is {total:.1f} GB, over {limit_gb:g} GB, and every other tree is in use', file=sys.stderr)


PRIORITY_AGING_SECONDS, PRIORITY_FLOOR = 120, 5  # the cargo shim's rank aging: a waiting ticket's rank improves by 1 per 120 s, never below 5 (ranks under 5 never age)


def priority_rank(environment=None):
    """The caller's rank in the build host's priority file (lines 'RANK GLOB', first glob matching the caller; lower is served first), as the cargo
    shim reads it: the caller is ST_AGENT, else CARGO_PRIORITY_CALLER (a run that carries only that one ranked 50 here while the shim ranked it
    right); the file is CARGO_PRIORITY_FILE or ~/.config/cargo-priority; 50 when unlisted or unreadable."""
    environment = os.environ if environment is None else environment
    caller = environment.get('ST_AGENT') or environment.get('CARGO_PRIORITY_CALLER', '')
    path = environment.get('CARGO_PRIORITY_FILE') or str(Path.home() / '.config/cargo-priority')
    try:
        lines = Path(path).read_text().splitlines()
    except OSError:
        lines = []
    for line in lines:
        fields = line.split('#')[0].split()
        if caller and len(fields) >= 2 and fields[0].isdigit() and fnmatch.fnmatchcase(caller, fields[1]):
            return int(fields[0])
    return 50


def process_started_ns(pid):
    """When process `pid` started, in epoch nanoseconds (the boot time plus /proc/PID/stat's start ticks, to about a second); None where /proc
    cannot tell."""
    try:
        stat = Path(f'/proc/{pid}/stat').read_text()
        ticks = int(stat[stat.rindex(')') + 2:].split()[19])  # field 22, counted after the command name, which may hold spaces
        boot = next(int(line.split()[1]) for line in Path('/proc/stat').read_text().splitlines() if line.startswith('btime '))
        return (boot * 10**9) + ticks * 10**9 // os.sysconf('SC_CLK_TCK')
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError, StopIteration):
        return None


def tree_queue(queue, now_ns):
    """The live tickets waiting for a pool tree (name RANK.NANOS.PID), best first: the shim's order, effective rank (aged) then arrival. A ticket
    whose process is gone is removed, and so is one whose pid now names a process that started after the ticket was taken (a waiter killed
    with SIGKILL leaves its ticket; a reused pid must not keep its place)."""
    found = []
    for name in os.listdir(queue):
        try:
            rank, nanos, pid = (int(part) for part in name.split('.'))
        except ValueError:
            continue
        try:
            os.kill(pid, 0)
        except ProcessLookupError:
            with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
                os.unlink(queue / name)
            continue
        except PermissionError:
            pass
        started = process_started_ns(pid)
        if started is not None and started > nanos + 2 * 10**9:  # the pid was reused by a process younger than the ticket: its waiter is gone
            with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
                os.unlink(queue / name)
            continue
        effective = rank if rank < PRIORITY_FLOOR else max(PRIORITY_FLOOR, rank - (now_ns - nanos) // (PRIORITY_AGING_SECONDS * 10**9))
        found.append((effective, nanos, name))
    return sorted(found)


def acquire_tree(root, size, rank=None, slot_ready=None, waits=None):
    """(n, lock): the lowest free tree of the pool, held by an flock until the lock is closed; waits while every tree is in use.
    Waiters queue in the build host's order, not by a race to the next free tree (2026-10-26: the CPU P0 builder, rank 3, waited two hours while
    rank-50 runs took each tree as it came free): each takes a ticket in queue/ and only the best live ticket tries the trees. `size` is the
    pool size or a function giving it, read again on every pass: when the host's heavy slots grow while runs wait, the best ticket takes a new
    tree at once instead of polling only the old ones (with every ticket behind it waiting too).
    With `slot_ready` (a function giving (ready, why): build_slot_ready, for a run that builds) a free tree is taken only when it says a build
    could start now, or once TREE_SLOT_WAIT_LIMIT seconds of a free tree left for want of a slot have passed (slot first, above). `waits`, a
    dict, is given the seconds spent: 'tree_wait_s' in all, and 'slot_wait_s' of them while a free tree waited for a build slot."""
    root.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    queue = root / 'queue'
    queue.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
    ticket = queue / f'{priority_rank() if rank is None else rank:03d}.{time.time_ns():019d}.{os.getpid()}'
    ticket.touch()
    waited, told = False, set()
    began, slot_waited, slot_seen, slot_told = time.monotonic(), 0.0, None, False

    def taken(n, lock):
        if waits is not None:
            waits.update(tree_wait_s=round(time.monotonic() - began), slot_wait_s=round(slot_waited))
        return n, lock

    try:
        while True:
            order = tree_queue(queue, time.time_ns())
            ahead = next((i for i, (_, _, name) in enumerate(order) if name == ticket.name), 0)
            trees = size() if callable(size) else size
            free_tree = False
            if ahead == 0:
                for n in range(trees):
                    lock = open(root / f'tree-{n}.lock', 'w')
                    try:
                        fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
                    except BlockingIOError:
                        lock.close()
                        continue
                    # A lease ends with the run that held it, not with every process it started (2026-10-28: a seat restart killed runs
                    # whose children went on). A free tree that still has processes at work in it is busy: never two writers in one tree.
                    users = tree_users(root / f'tree-{n}')
                    if not users and slot_ready is None:
                        return taken(n, lock)
                    if not users:
                        ready, why = slot_ready()
                        now = time.monotonic()
                        if slot_seen is not None:
                            slot_waited += now - slot_seen
                        if ready or slot_waited >= TREE_SLOT_WAIT_LIMIT:
                            if not ready:
                                print(f'fractal-test: taking tree-{n} after {round(slot_waited)}s without a free build slot ({why}; '
                                      f'TREE_SLOT_WAIT_LIMIT): its build waits for one in the tree', file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
                            elif slot_told:
                                print(f'fractal-test: a build slot is free ({why}): taking tree-{n} after {round(slot_waited)}s', file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
                            return taken(n, lock)
                        lock.close()
                        slot_seen, free_tree = now, True
                        if not slot_told:
                            slot_told = True
                            print(f'fractal-test: tree-{n} is free, but a build started now would wait for a slot ({why}); waiting for one '
                                  f'without holding a tree (at most {TREE_SLOT_WAIT_LIMIT}s)', file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
                        break  # every free tree would wait for the same slot
                    lock.close()
                    if n not in told:
                        told.add(n)
                        print(f'fractal-test: pool tree tree-{n} is free, but processes {", ".join(map(str, users))} still work in it (left by '
                              'a run that lost the tree); it is skipped until they end', file=sys.stderr)
            if not free_tree:
                slot_seen = None  # only the time a free tree waited for a slot counts toward TREE_SLOT_WAIT_LIMIT
                if not waited:
                    print(f'fractal-test: all {trees} pool trees are in use; waiting for one ({ahead} ahead in the build host\'s order)', file=sys.stderr)
                    waited = True
            time.sleep(POOL_WAIT_SECONDS)
    finally:
        with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
            ticket.unlink()


def tree_users(directory):
    """The live processes other than this one whose working directory is in the pool tree `directory`: work a run left there when it lost
    the tree's lease (its children, a lane's build in a scope of its own). Read from /proc; where there is none, nothing can be seen."""
    base = os.path.realpath(directory)
    found = []
    with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
        for entry in os.scandir('/proc'):
            if not entry.name.isdigit() or int(entry.name) == os.getpid():
                continue
            try:
                cwd = os.readlink(f'/proc/{entry.name}/cwd')
            except OSError:
                continue  # gone, or not ours to read
            if cwd == base or cwd.startswith(base + os.sep):
                found.append(int(entry.name))
    return sorted(found)


def clear_stale_index_lock(directory):
    """Remove the index.lock a run left in the pool tree `directory` when it died while git wrote the index (2026-10-28: a seat restart killed
    a run in its checkout of tree-2, and the next five runs given the tree failed on the lock). The caller holds the tree's lease and no
    process is left in the tree, so no git can be writing it. Logged; with a process still in the tree it is left alone and the run fails."""
    path = text(git('rev-parse', '--path-format=absolute', '--git-path', 'index.lock', cwd=directory, check=False))
    lock = Path(path) if path else None
    if lock is None or lock.name != 'index.lock' or not lock.exists():
        return
    users = tree_users(directory)
    if users:
        raise Failure(f'pool tree {directory} has an index.lock and processes {", ".join(map(str, users))} still in it: not taking it')
    lock.unlink(missing_ok=True)
    print(f'fractal-test: removed a stale {lock} (git was killed while writing it; no process is left in the tree)', file=sys.stderr,
          flush=True)


def is_pool_tree(directory):
    """Whether `directory` is a worktree of this repository (a leftover from another clone or a broken one is replaced)."""
    done = git('rev-parse', '--path-format=absolute', '--git-common-dir', cwd=directory, check=False)
    ours = git('rev-parse', '--path-format=absolute', '--git-common-dir', check=False)
    return done.returncode == 0 and ours.returncode == 0 and os.path.realpath(text(done)) == os.path.realpath(text(ours))


def carry_kept_inputs(old, new):
    """Move the inputs a pool tree keeps (POOL_KEEP) from the tree another clone left to the one just registered here, by rename (same disk)."""
    old, new = Path(old), Path(new)
    found = [name for name in ('repos', 'target/compat-baselines') if (old / name).is_dir()]
    for top, dirs, _ in os.walk(old):
        if 'node_modules' in dirs:
            found.append(os.path.relpath(os.path.join(top, 'node_modules'), old))
        # never look inside what is kept, a git directory or a build/store output
        dirs[:] = [name for name in dirs if name not in ('node_modules', '.git', 'target', 'buck-out', '.devenv', 'repos')]
    for relative in found:
        source, target = old / relative, new / relative
        if target.exists():
            continue  # tracked in this commit: the checkout's own is the truth
        target.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        try:
            os.rename(source, target)
        except OSError:
            pass  # a kept input that cannot move is simply fetched again


def prepare_tree(root, n, commit):
    """The pool tree `n`, at `commit` with nothing untracked or ignored left in it (build output lives outside, in target-N), or a
    Failure when it is still not clean: the signature is for the committed tree."""
    directory = root / f'tree-{n}'
    kept = None
    if directory.exists() and not is_pool_tree(directory):
        # Another clone's tree (or a broken one) sits at this slot's path. The path stays: the workspace crates' cargo ids contain it, so
        # keeping it keeps them warm. The tree is re-registered as a worktree of THIS clone (its notes land here), and the inputs the cleaning
        # keeps (members, installed dependencies, compat baselines) move across, so they are not fetched or installed again.
        kept = directory.with_name(directory.name + '.adopting')
        shutil.rmtree(kept, ignore_errors=True)
        os.rename(directory, kept)
    git('worktree', 'prune', check=False)
    if directory.exists():
        clear_stale_index_lock(directory)
        # Forced detached checkout replaces both index and tracked files, even after an interrupted run.
        # A separate reset --hard is redundant and is refused by agent git policies.
        git('checkout', '--detach', '-q', '-f', commit, cwd=directory)
    else:
        git('worktree', 'add', '--detach', '--force', '-q', str(directory), commit)
    if kept:
        carry_kept_inputs(kept, directory)
        subprocess.run(['chmod', '-R', 'u+rwX', '--', str(kept)], check=False, capture_output=True)
        shutil.rmtree(kept, ignore_errors=True)
    clean = ['clean', '-ffdxq', *[f'--exclude={pattern}' for pattern in POOL_KEEP]]
    if git(*clean, cwd=directory, check=False).returncode != 0:
        # A check can leave read-only directories (copies of nix store paths, under .devenv/ and repos/): make them writable, once.
        subprocess.run(['chmod', '-R', 'u+rwX', '--', str(directory)], check=False, capture_output=True)
        git(*clean, cwd=directory)
    # The tracked files must equal the commit (the signature is for it); what is kept above is ignored input, not a tracked file.
    dirty = text(git('status', '--porcelain', '--untracked-files=no', cwd=directory))
    if dirty:
        raise Failure(f'pool tree {directory} is not clean after resetting it to {commit[:12]}: {dirty.splitlines()[0]}')
    return directory


def members_command(directory):
    """CI's `megarepoApplyStep` (.github/workflows/workspace-ci.ts): `mr` from the flake's effect-utils pin. A test pins the two together."""
    rev = json.loads((directory / 'flake.lock').read_text())['nodes']['effect-utils']['locked']['rev']
    if not re.fullmatch(r'[0-9a-f]{40}', rev):
        raise ValueError(f'effect-utils rev {rev!r} is not a commit id')
    return ['nix', 'run', f'github:overengineeringstudio/effect-utils/{rev}#megarepo', '--',
            'apply', '--worktree-mode', 'commit', '--git-protocol', 'https']


# The member step fetches every member repository: minutes when they are cold, and it once sat 30 minutes behind a `git fetch` that was blocked
# writing to its parent (bun's `mr apply` did not read it) while the network was fine. A limit turns that into a refusal that says so.
MEMBERS_LIMIT = 1200


def members_limit():
    try:
        return max(1, int(POOL_SETTINGS.get('ST_TEST_MEMBERS_LIMIT', MEMBERS_LIMIT)))
    except ValueError:
        return MEMBERS_LIMIT


def materialize_members(directory):
    """Fill repos/ from the tree's megarepo.lock, as CI does before every lane that reads it; a tree without one needs nothing.

    Returns None, or why the members could not be materialized (then no check runs and nothing is signed). The command's output goes to a
    file, not a pipe this process would have to read, and a run past the limit is killed with everything it started."""
    if not (directory / 'megarepo.lock').is_file():
        return None
    try:
        command = members_command(directory)
    except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError, TypeError) as error:
        return f'cannot read the effect-utils pin from flake.lock: {error}'
    limit = members_limit()
    with tempfile.TemporaryFile() as output:
        process = subprocess.Popen(command, cwd=directory, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=output, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, start_new_session=True)
        try:
            code = process.wait(timeout=limit)
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            for sig in (signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGKILL):
                try:
                    os.killpg(process.pid, sig)
                except ProcessLookupError:
                    break
                try:
                    process.wait(timeout=10)
                    break
                except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                    continue
            output.seek(0, os.SEEK_END)
            output.seek(max(0, output.tell() - 4000))
            sys.stderr.write(output.read().decode(errors='replace'))
            return f'materializing the megarepo members timed out after {limit}s (ST_TEST_MEMBERS_LIMIT)'
        if code != 0:
            output.seek(0, os.SEEK_END)
            output.seek(max(0, output.tell() - 4000))
            sys.stderr.write(output.read().decode(errors='replace'))
            return f'materializing the megarepo members failed ({code})'
    return None


def reap_stale():
    """Remove worktrees an earlier fractal-test left when it was killed (no TERM, no cleanup): its scratch directory names its pid."""
    listing = text(git('worktree', 'list', '--porcelain', check=False))
    for block in listing.split('\n\n'):
        match = re.search(r'^worktree (\S*/st-test-[^/]+)/tree$', block, re.M)
        if not match:
            continue
        scratch = Path(match.group(1))
        try:
            os.kill(int((scratch / 'pid').read_text()), 0)
            continue  # still running
        except PermissionError:
            continue  # alive, and another user's: not ours to reap
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            pass
        git('worktree', 'remove', '--force', str(scratch / 'tree'), check=False)
        shutil.rmtree(scratch, ignore_errors=True)
    git('worktree', 'prune', check=False)


def ask_verifier(verifier, directory, tree, check, base, repository, lane_base=None, crate_key=None):
    """(covered, why, record): the verifier's answer for `check` on `tree`; record is the covering note's record id, or None."""
    command = [sys.executable, verifier, '--repo', str(directory), '--tree', tree, '--check', check, '--keys-from', base]
    if crate_key:
        command += ['--crate-key', crate_key]
    if repository:
        command += ['--policy-from', base, '--repository', repository]
        if lane_base and b'--lane-base' in Path(verifier).read_bytes():
            command += ['--lane-base', lane_base]
    if b'--run-input' in Path(verifier).read_bytes():
        command += run_input_arguments(check, base)
    done = subprocess.run(command, capture_output=True, text=True)
    try:
        result = json.loads(done.stdout)
    except ValueError:
        return False, 'verifier gave no answer: ' + (done.stderr.strip().splitlines() or ['-'])[-1][:120], None
    if done.returncode == 0:
        return True, f'covered by {result.get("kind")} {result.get("record", "")[:12]}', result.get('record')
    return False, result.get('reason') or 'not covered', None


def covered(verifier, directory, tree, check, base, repository, lane_base=None, crate_key=None):
    return ask_verifier(verifier, directory, tree, check, base, repository, lane_base, crate_key)[:2]


def push_record(directory, remote, tree, record):
    """Push the tag and note of an existing record (as evidence-wrap pushes a new one: the token travels in the environment, never in an
    argument). Returns None, or why it failed. A record with no local refs came from the remote: nothing to push."""
    refs = [ref for ref in (f'refs/tags/evidence/{tree}/{record}', f'refs/notes/evidence/{tree}/{record}')
            if git('rev-parse', '--verify', '-q', ref, cwd=directory, check=False).returncode == 0]
    if not refs:
        return None
    extra = {}
    token = os.environ.get('EVIDENCE_PUSH_TOKEN', '')
    if token:
        header = base64.b64encode(f'x-access-token:{token}'.encode()).decode()
        count = int(os.environ.get('GIT_CONFIG_COUNT', '0'))
        extra = {'GIT_CONFIG_COUNT': str(count + 1), f'GIT_CONFIG_KEY_{count}': 'http.extraheader',
                 f'GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_{count}': f'Authorization: Basic {header}'}
    pushed = git('push', '--quiet', remote, *[f'{ref}:{ref}' for ref in refs], cwd=directory, env=extra, check=False)
    if pushed.returncode != 0:
        message = pushed.stderr.decode(errors='replace')
        return message.replace(token, '***').strip()[-400:] if token else message.strip()[-400:]
    return None


LOG_UNIT = 'evidence-log.service'


def log_nudge_argv():
    """The command that asks this host's evidence log to pull now, or None when the host runs no log."""
    configured = os.environ.get('ST_EVIDENCE_LOG_NUDGE')
    if configured:
        argv = json.loads(configured)
        if not isinstance(argv, list) or not argv or not all(isinstance(a, str) for a in argv):
            raise ValueError('ST_EVIDENCE_LOG_NUDGE must be a JSON array of strings')
        return argv
    if (Path.home() / '.config/systemd/user' / LOG_UNIT).exists() and shutil.which('systemctl'):
        return ['systemctl', '--user', 'start', '--no-block', LOG_UNIT]
    return None


def nudge_log():
    """One line for the summary, or None when there is no log to ask. Best effort: the pull's own timer runs anyway."""
    try:
        argv = log_nudge_argv()
    except ValueError as error:
        return f'evidence log: not asked ({error})'
    if argv is None:
        return None
    try:
        done = subprocess.run(argv, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, capture_output=True, timeout=15)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as error:
        return f'evidence log: could not ask it to pull now ({error}); its timer will'
    if done.returncode != 0:
        return f'evidence log: asking it to pull now failed ({done.stderr.decode(errors="replace").strip()[-200:]}); its timer will'
    return 'evidence log: asked this host\'s log to pull now'


def repository_of(remote):
    url = text(git('remote', 'get-url', remote, check=False))
    for prefix in ('https://github.com/', 'git@github.com:', 'ssh://git@github.com/'):
        if url.startswith(prefix):
            return url[len(prefix):].removesuffix('.git').strip('/')
    return None


RUST_TEST_KEYS = 'scripts/rust-test-keys'
RUST_TEST_INPUTS = '.st/rust-test-inputs.json'


def only_crates(keys, names):
    """(keys narrowed to `names`, or None and why a name is not a workspace crate of this tree)."""
    unknown = [name for name in names if name not in keys]
    if unknown:
        return None, f'not a workspace crate of this tree: {", ".join(unknown)}'
    return ({name: keys[name] for name in names} if names else keys), None


def judged_by_policy(base):
    """Whether the accepted base's skip policy names rust-tests; until it does (docs/per-crate-test-signing.md, step 3) a note counts by its
    signature, crate and key alone, which is all this machine's own skip decision needs."""
    return 'rust-tests' in policy_checks(base)


def crate_keys(base, directory, tree, scratch):
    """{crate: key} for `tree`, from the accepted base's scripts/rust-test-keys and inputs file (a verifier must use the base's). Only
    while the base's policy does not name rust-tests, so nothing judges a note yet, are the tree's own used: the change that introduces them
    is judged by itself. Once the base's policy names it, a base without them is an error, never a fallback. Returns (keys, error)."""
    tool = Path(scratch) / 'rust-test-keys'
    shown = git('show', f'{base}:{RUST_TEST_KEYS}', check=False)
    inputs_at = base
    if shown.returncode == 0 and git('cat-file', '-e', f'{base}:{RUST_TEST_INPUTS}', check=False).returncode == 0:
        tool.write_bytes(shown.stdout)
    elif judged_by_policy(base):
        return None, f'the accepted base judges rust-tests but has no {RUST_TEST_KEYS} and {RUST_TEST_INPUTS}: only the base\'s tool and inputs may key a note'
    else:
        tool.write_bytes((Path(directory) / RUST_TEST_KEYS).read_bytes())
        inputs_at = tree
    verifier = Path(scratch) / 'evidence-verify'
    verifier.write_bytes(git('show', f'{base}:scripts/evidence-verify').stdout)
    done = subprocess.run([sys.executable, str(tool), '--repo', str(directory), '--tree', tree, '--inputs-from', inputs_at,
                           '--verifier', str(verifier), '--json'], capture_output=True, text=True)
    if done.returncode != 0:
        return None, done.stderr.strip()[-300:] or 'rust-test-keys failed'
    return json.loads(done.stdout), None


def fetch_crate_notes(remote, keys):
    """The per-crate records indexed under these keys, from the remote in one fetch (best effort: a remote without them is no error)."""
    if not remote:
        return
    refspecs = []
    for key in keys.values():
        if key:
            refspecs += [f'+refs/notes/evidence-input/rust-tests/{key}/*:refs/notes/evidence-input/rust-tests/{key}/*',
                         f'+refs/tags/evidence/input/rust-tests/{key}/*:refs/tags/evidence/input/rust-tests/{key}/*']
    for start in range(0, len(refspecs), 200):
        git('fetch', '-q', '--no-tags', remote, *refspecs[start:start + 200], check=False)


def members_missing(verifier, directory, tree, base, repository, lane_base):
    """{crate: reason} for the workspace members with no satisfying note, from ONE verifier run (`--members`: one process reads each record once,
    where 52 `--crate-key` runs each read all 52 of the tree's records); None when the verifier cannot say (the caller asks crate by crate)."""
    command = [sys.executable, verifier, '--repo', str(directory), '--tree', tree, '--check', 'rust-tests', '--keys-from', base, '--members',
               '--policy-from', base, '--repository', repository]
    if lane_base and b'--lane-base' in Path(verifier).read_bytes():
        command += ['--lane-base', lane_base]
    done = subprocess.run(command, capture_output=True, text=True)
    try:
        result = json.loads(done.stdout)
    except ValueError:
        return None
    if result.get('satisfied'):
        return {}
    return result.get('missing') if result.get('reason') == 'crate_uncovered' and isinstance(result.get('missing'), dict) else None


def uncovered_crates(verifier, directory, tree, base, repository, lane_base, keys):
    """[(crate, reason)] for the crates with no passing note for their key, and the number of covered ones."""
    missing, have = [], 0
    if repository and keys and all(keys.values()):
        batched = members_missing(verifier, directory, tree, base, repository, lane_base)
        if batched is not None:
            # --members judges every workspace member; `keys` may be narrowed to some of them (--crate).
            missing = [(crate, batched[crate]) for crate in sorted(keys) if crate in batched]
            return missing, len(keys) - len(missing)
    for crate, key in sorted(keys.items()):
        if not key:
            missing.append((crate, 'no declared path exists in this tree'))
            continue
        ok, why = covered(verifier, directory, tree, 'rust-tests', base, repository, lane_base, crate_key=f'{crate}={key}')
        if ok:
            have += 1
        else:
            missing.append((crate, why))
    return missing, have


def listed_no_tests(base):
    """The crates the accepted base lists under no_tests in .st/rust-test-inputs.json (reviewed: they have no test to run); empty when unreadable."""
    done = git('show', f'{base}:{RUST_TEST_INPUTS}', check=False)
    try:
        listed = json.loads(done.stdout.decode()).get('no_tests', []) if done.returncode == 0 else []
    except (ValueError, AttributeError, UnicodeDecodeError):
        return []
    return [name for name in listed if isinstance(name, str)] if isinstance(listed, list) else []


PUSH_CHUNK = 40  # refs per `git push`: a rejected or failed ref is retried on its own, and no one request carries hundreds
PUSH_TRIES = 3


def push_records(directory, remote, refs):
    """Push every record's refs (the same refs evidence-wrap --push sends for one record), in chunks, retrying the ones that failed.
    git's porcelain output says which ref failed and why; a transient failure (one 76-ref push once reported only `(failed)` for one ref, and
    the ref pushed alone afterwards) is retried up to PUSH_TRIES times with a pause. Returns an error text naming the refs left, or None."""
    token = os.environ.get('EVIDENCE_PUSH_TOKEN', '')
    environment = dict(os.environ)
    if token:  # the same header evidence-wrap sets; it travels in the environment, never in an argument
        header = base64.b64encode(f'x-access-token:{token}'.encode()).decode()
        count = int(environment.get('GIT_CONFIG_COUNT', '0'))
        environment.update({'GIT_CONFIG_COUNT': str(count + 1), f'GIT_CONFIG_KEY_{count}': 'http.extraheader',
                            f'GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_{count}': f'Authorization: Basic {header}'})
    pending, reasons = list(refs), {}
    for attempt in range(PUSH_TRIES):
        if attempt:
            time.sleep(2 * attempt)
        failed = []
        for start in range(0, len(pending), PUSH_CHUNK):
            chunk = pending[start:start + PUSH_CHUNK]
            done = subprocess.run(['git', '-C', str(directory), 'push', '--porcelain', remote, *(f'{ref}:{ref}' for ref in chunk)],
                                  capture_output=True, text=True, env=environment)
            if done.returncode == 0:
                continue
            rejected = {}
            for line in done.stdout.splitlines():
                if line.startswith('!'):
                    fields = line.split('\t')
                    if len(fields) >= 2:
                        rejected[fields[1].split(':')[0]] = fields[2] if len(fields) > 2 else 'rejected'
            if not rejected:  # nothing was said per ref (a transport error): the whole chunk goes round again
                lines = (done.stderr.replace(token, '***') if token else done.stderr).strip().splitlines()
                rejected = {ref: (lines[0][:120] if lines else 'push failed') for ref in chunk}
            failed += [ref for ref in chunk if ref in rejected]
            reasons.update(rejected)
        pending = failed
        if not pending:
            return None
    shown = ', '.join(f'{ref} ({reasons.get(ref, "failed")[:120]})' for ref in pending[:3])
    return f'push of {len(pending)} of {len(refs)} refs failed after {PUSH_TRIES} tries: {shown}' + (' ...' if len(pending) > 3 else '')


def sign_jobs():
    """How many crate signatures run at once (ST_TEST_SIGN_JOBS; default 8)."""
    try:
        return max(1, int(POOL_SETTINGS.get('ST_TEST_SIGN_JOBS', '8')))
    except ValueError:
        return 8


def shared_toolchain(wrap_path):
    """The dev shell digest evidence-wrap would compute for every signature, once; None when it cannot (each wrap then computes its own)."""
    try:
        loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader('evidence_wrap_for_st_test', wrap_path)
        module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(importlib.util.spec_from_loader(loader.name, loader))
        loader.exec_module(module)
        return module.toolchain_digest(None)
    except Exception:  # an unreadable script, or nix cannot name the shell: the wrapper reports it for itself
        return None


def filterset_input(selected):
    """The expression scripts/ci-linux builds from CI_TEST_PACKAGES, as the record's `filterset` input: the whole expression while the signer
    takes it (an input is at most 500 characters), else the count and the sha256 of the expression, which still pins the exact crate list."""
    expression = ' or '.join(f'package(={crate})' for crate in selected)
    if len(expression) <= FILTERSET_LIMIT:
        return expression
    return f'{len(selected)} packages sha256:{hashlib.sha256(expression.encode()).hexdigest()}'


def usable_report(path):
    """Whether `path` is a nextest JUnit report that was written whole (nextest writes it when the run ends)."""
    try:
        return path is not None and path.exists() and ET.parse(path).getroot().tag == 'testsuites'
    except (OSError, ET.ParseError):
        return False


def env_skips_file(scratch, check):
    """The file a check's tests append to when this host cannot run them (CI_TEST_ENV_SKIPS): one 'CRATE TEST' line per skip."""
    return Path(scratch) / f'env-skips-{check}.txt'


def crate_env_skips(path, crate):
    """The env_skips input of one crate's record: its skipped tests, comma-joined, or 'none'."""
    lines = path.read_text().splitlines() if path.exists() else []
    value = ','.join(sorted({line.strip() for line in lines if line.strip().split(' ', 1)[0] == crate})) or 'none'
    # The signer takes inputs of at most 500 characters, as evidence-wrap --input-file records a longer list.
    return value if len(value) <= 500 else f'{value.count(",") + 1} entries, sha256 {hashlib.sha256(value.encode()).hexdigest()}'


def takes_input_files(wrapper):
    """Whether the base's evidence-wrap records --input-file (older bases' wrappers refuse the flag)."""
    return '--input-file' in Path(wrapper).read_text()


UNCOVERED = 'not signed'  # a crate left for CI (a test passed only on a retry): its row, and the check's unsigned flag


def unsettled_tests(directory, junit, crate):
    """(failed, retried): the crate's tests that failed every try and those that passed only on a retry in the report
    (scripts/ci-rust-test-result's `FAIL` and `flaky` lines)."""
    done = subprocess.run([sys.executable, str(directory / 'scripts/ci-rust-test-result')], capture_output=True, text=True,
                          env=dict(os.environ, RUST_TEST_JUNIT=str(junit), RUST_TEST_CRATE=crate))
    lines = [line.split(' ', 1) for line in done.stdout.splitlines() if ' ' in line]
    return [name for status, name in lines if status == 'FAIL'], [name for status, name in lines if status == 'flaky']


def name_tests(names):
    """At most two test names without their binary, for a summary row."""
    return ', '.join(name.split(' ', 1)[-1] for name in names[:2]) + (' ...' if len(names) > 2 else '')


def run_rust_tests(args, directory, tools, scratch, tree, commit, base_commit, lane_base, repository, signer, lanes, target, report=None):
    """The `rust-tests` check: one nextest run of the crates that have no passing note for their key, on the workspace build CI makes,
    then one signature per crate over that crate's part of the run. Returns the summary rows.

    With `report` (the JUnit file of linux-gate's own run in this invocation: the same workspace build, the whole selection) nothing is run again:
    the crates without a note are signed from it, and the records say so (filterset `workspace`, the command of linux-gate's run)."""
    begun = time.monotonic()
    keys, error = crate_keys(args.base, directory, tree, scratch)
    if not error:
        keys, error = only_crates(keys, args.crate)
    if error:
        return [('rust-tests', 'NOT RUN', 0, f'cannot compute the per-crate keys: {error}')], True
    if args.out:
        missing, have = list(keys.items()), 0  # Export the whole selection, even when local notes already cover it.
        keyed = time.monotonic()
    else:
        fetch_crate_notes(args.push or 'origin', keys)
        keyed = time.monotonic()
        missing, have = uncovered_crates(tools['evidence-verify'], directory, tree, args.base, repository if judged_by_policy(args.base) else None,
                                         lane_base, keys)
    verified_at = time.monotonic()
    if not missing:
        return [('rust-tests', 'covered', round(verified_at - begun),
                 f'all {len(keys)} crates already have a passing note for their key (keys {round(keyed - begun)}s, verify {round(verified_at - keyed)}s)')], False
    selected = [crate for crate, _ in missing]
    if usable_report(report):
        junit, ran_seconds = report, 0
        skips = env_skips_file(scratch, 'linux-gate')  # the tests of linux-gate's run wrote the report and their skips
        filterset = 'workspace'
        run_sha = hashlib.sha256(json.dumps([LOCAL['linux-gate']]).encode()).hexdigest()
    else:
        junit = Path(scratch) / 'nextest-junit.xml'
        environment = dict(os.environ) if os.environ.get('ST_EVIDENCE_PATH') == 'keep' else dict(os.environ, PATH=check_path())
        skips = env_skips_file(scratch, RUST_TESTS)
        environment.update(CI_TEST_PACKAGES=','.join(selected), CI_TEST_JUNIT=str(junit), CI_TEST_ENV_SKIPS=str(skips))
        environment.setdefault('CARGO_INCREMENTAL', '0')  # as the other checks: no incremental state in a one-shot build
        environment.pop('BUILD_ADMISSION_CLASS', None)  # it builds: never a light start
        if target:
            environment['CARGO_TARGET_DIR'] = str(target)
        started = time.monotonic()
        limit = Limit(args.time_limit, Path(scratch) / 'rust-tests.queue', args.queue_limit)
        environment['CI_LOCAL_QUEUE_LOG'] = str(limit.queue_log)
        code = run_limited(governed(RUN_TESTS, 'rust-tests'), limit, cwd=directory, env=environment)
        ran_seconds = round(time.monotonic() - started)
        if code not in RUN_COMPLETED or not junit.exists():
            return [('rust-tests', 'timed out' if code == 124 else 'NOT SIGNED', ran_seconds,
                     f'the run did not complete (exit {code}{"" if junit.exists() else ", no report"}): no crate was signed')], True
        # The expression scripts/ci-linux builds from CI_TEST_PACKAGES: the record says which crates the run was filtered to.
        filterset = filterset_input(selected)
        run_sha = hashlib.sha256(json.dumps([RUN_TESTS, selected]).encode()).hexdigest()
    rows, failed, signed_count, empty = [], False, 0, []
    signing_from = time.monotonic()
    # The dev shell's digest is the same for every crate: name it once (a nix evaluation of seconds) rather than once per signature.
    toolchain = args.toolchain_sha256 or shared_toolchain(tools['evidence-wrap'])

    def sign(crate):
        """(row, refspecs): a summary row when the crate was not signed, else None and the refs of its record, which are pushed together
        afterwards: its facts out of the report, then one wrapped, signed and locally verified record."""
        facts = json.loads(subprocess.run([sys.executable, str(directory / 'scripts/ci-rust-test-result'), '--summary'], capture_output=True,
                                          text=True, env=dict(os.environ, RUST_TEST_JUNIT=str(junit), RUST_TEST_CRATE=crate)).stdout or 'null')
        if not facts:
            return (f'rust-tests:{crate}', 'NOT SIGNED', 0, 'cannot read its result from the report'), []
        failing, flaky = unsettled_tests(directory, junit, crate)
        if flaky and not failing:
            # As CI's per-crate notes (scripts/ci-rust-test-notes): a crate with a test that passed only on a retry is not signed, so it
            # stays uncovered and CI runs it. Not a failure of this run. A test that failed every try is one, whatever else was retried:
            # the crate goes on to its failing record below.
            return (f'rust-tests:{crate}', UNCOVERED, 0, f'{len(flaky)} test(s) passed only on a retry, CI runs it: {name_tests(flaky)}'[:200]), UNCOVERED
        wrap = [sys.executable, tools['evidence-wrap'], '--check', 'rust-tests', '--attempt', args.attempt,
                '--signer', signer, '--verifier', tools['evidence-verify'], '--keys-from', args.base,
                '--input', f'crate={crate}', '--input', f'source_inputs={keys[crate]}', '--input', f'tests_run={facts["tests_run"]}',
                '--input', f'tests_ignored={facts["tests_ignored"]}', '--input', f'test_list_sha256={facts["test_list_sha256"]}',
                '--input', f'ignored_list_sha256={facts["ignored_list_sha256"]}',
                '--input', f'filterset={filterset}', '--input', f'run_command_sha256={run_sha}',
                # A test this host cannot run returns early and the report counts it as passed: the record names it.
                '--input', f'env_skips={crate_env_skips(skips, crate)}']
        if RUST_TESTS in lanes:
            wrap += ['--lanes-from', lane_base]
        if toolchain:
            wrap += ['--toolchain-sha256', toolchain]
        if args.out:
            wrap += ['--out', args.out]
        started = time.monotonic()
        command = wrap + ['--'] + RUST_TESTS_COMMAND
        print(f'fractal-test: evidence-wrap argv: {shlex.join(command)}', file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
        signed = subprocess.run(command, cwd=directory, env=dict(os.environ, RUST_TEST_JUNIT=str(junit), RUST_TEST_CRATE=crate),
                                stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.PIPE, text=True)
        if signed.returncode != 0:
            detail = (f'{len(failing)} test(s) failed every try: {name_tests(failing)}' if failing else
                      (signed.stderr.strip().splitlines() or ['failed'])[-1])[:160]
            return (f'rust-tests:{crate}', f'FAILED ({signed.returncode})', round(time.monotonic() - started), detail), None
        try:
            record = json.loads(signed.stderr[signed.stderr.index('{'):])
            if args.out:
                # Export summaries have no refs or verification verdict: the pack has not been imported.
                if record['check'] != RUST_TESTS or record['tree'] != tree or record['exit_code'] != 0:
                    raise ValueError('the export summary does not match the successful run')
                refs = []
            else:
                if not record.get('verified'):
                    raise ValueError('the record did not verify locally')
                key = keys[crate]
                refs = [record['tag'], record['ref'], f'refs/tags/evidence/input/rust-tests/{key}/{record["record"]}',
                        f'refs/notes/evidence-input/rust-tests/{key}/{record["record"]}']
        except (ValueError, KeyError) as error:
            return (f'rust-tests:{crate}', 'FAILED (record)', round(time.monotonic() - started), f'cannot read the signed record: {error}'[:160]), None
        return None, (refs, facts)

    # Each signature is a verifier run, a signer run and a push of refs of its own, a dozen seconds on a repository with many records:
    # serial, 52 crates took as long as the tests. They are independent (distinct refs), so they run side by side.
    # The first goes alone: the signer creates its scratch repository when it is missing, and two signers racing to create it fail one.
    outcomes = [sign(selected[0])]
    with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=sign_jobs()) as pool:
        outcomes += list(pool.map(sign, selected[1:]))
    pushable = []
    for crate, (row, signed) in zip(selected, outcomes):
        if row:
            rows.append(row)
            failed = failed or signed != UNCOVERED
        else:
            refs, facts = signed
            signed_count += 1
            pushable += refs
            if not int(facts['tests_run']):
                empty.append(crate)
    pushing_from = time.monotonic()
    if args.push and pushable:
        pushed = push_records(directory, args.push, pushable)
        if pushed:
            rows.append(('rust-tests:push', 'FAILED', 0, pushed))
            failed = True
    detail = (f'{signed_count} of {len(selected)} crates signed from linux-gate\'s own nextest run ({have} of {len(keys)} already had a passing note)'
              if junit == report else
              f'{signed_count} of {len(selected)} crates run and signed ({have} of {len(keys)} already had a passing note); one nextest run on the workspace build')
    if args.out:
        detail += '; packs exported, not imported or verified'
    left = [crate for crate, (_, signed) in zip(selected, outcomes) if signed == UNCOVERED]
    if left:
        detail += f'; {len(left)} left for CI, a test passed only on a retry: {", ".join(left[:8])}' + (' ...' if len(left) > 8 else '')
    empty = [crate for crate in empty if crate not in listed_no_tests(args.base)]
    if empty:
        # A note that ran no test covers nothing once a policy judges it: the crate belongs under no_tests in .st/rust-test-inputs.json (reviewed).
        detail += f'; {len(empty)} ran no test (list under no_tests if they have none): {", ".join(empty[:8])}' + (' ...' if len(empty) > 8 else '')
    ended = time.monotonic()
    # The row's time is all of it, not only the tests: the keys, the verification of every crate, the signing and the push were 0 s here once.
    detail += (f' [keys {round(keyed - begun)}s, verify {round(verified_at - keyed)}s, tests {ran_seconds}s, sign {round(pushing_from - signing_from)}s, '
               f'push {round(ended - pushing_from)}s]')
    rows.insert(0, ('rust-tests', 'passed' if not failed else 'FAILED', round(ended - begun), detail))
    return rows, failed


# scripts/ci-local's closing stage table (one line per stage it ran: name, start, end, seconds, and for a stage its exit code and the build
# queue it waited in), as `printf '%-26s %s  %s  %6ss  exit %-3s queue: %s waits, %ss'` prints it after the `ci-local:` prefix; setup has no exit.
CI_LOCAL = 'scripts/ci-local'
STAGE_LINE = re.compile(r'ci-local:\s+(\S+)\s+(\d{4}-\d\d-\d\dT\d\d:\d\d:\d\dZ)\s+(\d{4}-\d\d-\d\dT\d\d:\d\d:\d\dZ)\s+(\d+)s'
                        r'(?:\s+exit\s+(\S+)(?:\s+queue:\s+(\d+)\s+waits,\s+(\d+)s)?)?\s*')
# How long a check's output is still copied after the check itself exited: a process it left behind may hold the pipe open.
STREAM_GRACE = 5
QUEUE_LIMIT = 14400  # default --queue-limit: the build-slot waits a check may spend, apart from its --time-limit
LIMIT_POLL = 1.0  # how often a check's limit is read again while it runs
# scripts/ci-local prints this once its own host-load gate has let it start, before its setup: a ci-local check's --time-limit counts from
# here. Before it the check waits (build admission for its `nix develop`, the dev shell, ci-local's load gate) and only SILENCE_LIMIT bounds it.
CI_LOCAL_STARTED = b'ci-local: stage logs in '
SILENCE_LIMIT = 1800  # seconds without any output, before a ci-local check has started, after which it is stopped (a waiting build-admission prints every 60 s)


def queue_spans(path, now=None):
    """[(stage, start, end)]: the build-slot waits scripts/ci-local wrote to `path` (CI_LOCAL_QUEUE_LOG: `wait|slot EPOCH STAGE` lines); a
    wait not yet ended runs to `now`. A missing or unreadable file has none."""
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    try:
        lines = Path(path).read_text(errors='replace').splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return []
    waiting, spans = {}, []
    for line in lines:
        parts = line.split(' ', 2)
        if len(parts) != 3:
            continue
        kind, at, stage = parts
        try:
            at = float(at)
        except ValueError:
            continue
        if kind == 'wait':
            waiting.setdefault(stage, at)
        elif kind == 'slot' and stage in waiting:
            spans.append((stage, waiting.pop(stage), at))
    return spans + [(stage, start, now) for stage, start in waiting.items()]


def merged_spans(spans):
    """`spans` ((start, end) pairs) as the sorted, disjoint spans of their union."""
    out = []
    for start, end in sorted(spans):
        if end <= start:
            continue
        if out and start <= out[-1][1]:
            out[-1][1] = max(out[-1][1], end)
        else:
            out.append([start, end])
    return out


def queued_seconds(path, now=None):
    """Seconds of the build-slot waits scripts/ci-local wrote to `path` (queue_spans) as the union of their spans, so two stages waiting at
    the same time count once; a wait not yet ended runs to `now`. A missing or unreadable file is 0."""
    return sum(end - start for start, end in merged_spans((start, end) for _, start, end in queue_spans(path, now)))


def blocked_seconds(path, stages, now=None):
    """Seconds in which scripts/ci-local held its tree and built nothing: some stage waited for a build slot (the queue log `path`) while no
    stage was running outside its own waits (the stage spans of its closing table, `stages`, as ci_local_stages reads them). Two stages run at
    once, so one stage's wait while another builds is not counted. A stage whose times cannot be read counts as not running."""
    waits = queue_spans(path, now)
    running = []
    for stage in stages:
        try:
            start, end = (calendar.timegm(time.strptime(stage[key], '%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ')) for key in ('start', 'end'))
        except (KeyError, TypeError, ValueError):
            continue
        cursor = start
        for wait_start, wait_end in merged_spans((a, b) for name, a, b in waits if name == stage['name']):
            if wait_start > cursor:
                running.append((cursor, min(wait_start, end)))
            cursor = max(cursor, wait_end)
        running.append((cursor, end))
    waited, ran = merged_spans((a, b) for _, a, b in waits), merged_spans(running)
    total, i = 0.0, 0
    for start, end in waited:  # each wait, less the parts in which a stage was running
        total += end - start
        while i < len(ran) and ran[i][1] <= start:
            i += 1
        j = i
        while j < len(ran) and ran[j][0] < end:
            total -= min(end, ran[j][1]) - max(start, ran[j][0])
            j += 1
    return total


class Limit:
    """A check's time limit that counts only the time it runs: `seconds` of running, plus the build-slot waits read from `queue_log`
    (queued_seconds), of which at most `queue_limit` seconds in all.

    With `begins_at` (bytes the check prints once it really starts, CI_LOCAL_STARTED) the running time counts only from that output on
    (`output` is given every chunk the check prints): what comes before it is waiting, admission for its dev shell included, which no
    queue log records (2026-10-27: a linux-gate spent its whole 7200 s before ci-local started and was stopped with its tests passing). Until
    then `silence` seconds without any output stop it (`silent` is then True), and so does waiting longer than `queue_limit` in all (`waited_out`):
    a wedged admission prints every 60 s and is never silent. `pre_start` is the waiting time."""

    def __init__(self, seconds, queue_log=None, queue_limit=QUEUE_LIMIT, begins_at=None, silence=None):
        self.seconds, self.queue_log, self.queue_limit = seconds, queue_log, queue_limit
        self.started = self.heard = time.monotonic()
        self.begins_at, self.tail, self.pre_start, self.silent, self.waited_out = begins_at, b'', 0.0, False, False
        self.silence = SILENCE_LIMIT if silence is None else silence

    def output(self, chunk):
        """The check printed `chunk`: it is not silent, and when `begins_at` is in it, its running time starts now."""
        now = self.heard = time.monotonic()
        if self.begins_at:
            seen = self.tail + chunk
            if self.begins_at in seen:
                self.pre_start, self.started, self.begins_at = now - self.started, now, None
            else:
                self.tail = seen[-len(self.begins_at):]

    def queued(self):
        return queued_seconds(self.queue_log) if self.queue_log else 0.0

    def remaining(self):
        """Seconds left before the check is over its limit (0 or less once it is): running time or waiting time, whichever ends first."""
        if self.begins_at:  # not started yet: silence ends it, and so does a wait past queue_limit
            now = time.monotonic()
            quiet, waiting = self.silence - (now - self.heard), self.queue_limit - (now - self.started)
            self.silent, self.waited_out = quiet <= 0, waiting <= 0 < quiet
            return min(quiet, waiting)
        queued = self.queued()
        ran = time.monotonic() - self.started - queued
        return min(self.seconds - ran, self.queue_limit - queued)

    def waiting(self):
        """Seconds the check has waited before it started: so far, while it has not."""
        return time.monotonic() - self.started if self.begins_at else self.pre_start


def wait_limited(process, limit):
    """`process`'s exit code, or 124 after killing it (the process itself, as subprocess.run's timeout does) once `limit` has run out."""
    while True:
        left = limit.remaining()
        if left <= 0:
            process.kill()
            process.wait()
            return 124
        try:
            return process.wait(timeout=min(left, LIMIT_POLL))
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            continue


def run_limited(argv, limit, **options):
    """subprocess.run(argv, **options).returncode under `limit` (a Limit): 124 when it ran out. Any error here kills the process."""
    with subprocess.Popen(argv, **options) as process:
        try:
            return wait_limited(process, limit)
        except BaseException:
            process.kill()
            raise


def queue_note(limit):
    """What the summary row adds about the check's waits before it started and for build slots, or ''."""
    queued, waited = round(limit.queued()), round(limit.waiting())
    notes = ([f'stopped after {limit.silence:g}s without output before ci-local started'] if limit.silent else []) + \
            ([f'stopped after waiting {limit.queue_limit:g}s (--queue-limit) before ci-local started'] if limit.waited_out else []) + \
            ([f'waited {waited}s before ci-local started (admission, dev shell)'] if waited else []) + \
            ([f'waited {queued}s for build slots'] if queued else [])
    return f'; {", ".join(notes)} (not counted against --time-limit)' if notes else ''


def ci_local_stages(path):
    """The stage rows of scripts/ci-local's table in `path` (a check's whole output), in order; [] when it printed none (before #3718) or the
    file cannot be read. A stage's exit and queue fields are None when the line has none (setup) or they are not numbers."""
    def number(value):
        return int(value) if value is not None and re.fullmatch(r'-?\d+', value) else None
    stages = []
    try:
        with open(path, errors='replace') as lines:
            for line in lines:
                match = STAGE_LINE.fullmatch(line.rstrip('\r\n').split('\r')[-1])
                if match:
                    name, start, end, seconds, code, waits, queued = match.groups()
                    stages.append({'name': name, 'start': start, 'end': end, 'seconds': int(seconds), 'exit': number(code),
                                   'queue_waits': number(waits), 'queue_s': number(queued)})
    except OSError:
        return []
    return stages


def pre_start_fifo(path):
    """`path` made a FIFO (anything there before removed) for the input pre_start_s, so that what evidence-wrap reads from it is ordered after
    every line of the check's output: see answer_pre_start."""
    with contextlib.suppress(FileNotFoundError):
        os.unlink(path)
    os.mkfifo(path, 0o600)
    return path


def answer_pre_start(fifo, pipe, take, limit):
    """Write the input pre_start_s to `fifo` once evidence-wrap opens it to read: True then, False while no one has it open to read.

    evidence-wrap reads its --input-file inputs only after the check ended, and only after it copied all the check's output to `pipe`
    (flushed). So when its open of the FIFO has begun, every line ci-local printed is already in `pipe`: drain it (`take` each chunk, which
    is how the Limit hears ci-local start), then write the seconds the check waited before ci-local started, or nothing when it never did
    (the input is then 'none'). A plain file, written when this process happened to read the start line, raced the wrapper's read and lost on
    a loaded host: the note said 'none' for a check that had started (2026-10-28, merge-queue ejections). The open is non-blocking, so with
    no reader yet it fails at once (ENXIO) and the caller tries again on its next pass; the write is blocking, so the wrapper reads it whole."""
    try:
        handle = os.open(fifo, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_NONBLOCK)
    except OSError as error:
        if error.errno in (errno.ENXIO, errno.ENOENT):
            return False
        raise
    try:
        while select.select([pipe], [], [], 0)[0]:
            chunk = os.read(pipe, 65536)
            if not chunk:
                break
            take(chunk)
        os.set_blocking(handle, True)
        if limit.begins_at is None:
            os.write(handle, f'{round(limit.pre_start)}\n'.encode())
    finally:
        os.close(handle)
    return True


def streamed(argv, log, timeout, pre_start=None, **options):
    """`argv`'s exit code (124 once `timeout`, seconds or a Limit, has run out), with its output (stdout and stderr, as one) copied as it comes both to this terminal, unchanged,
    and to the file `log`, so the summary can read what it printed. subprocess.run's own semantics otherwise: past the timeout the process
    itself is killed (not its group: what it started is left as before) and waited for, and any error here (TERM's exit included) kills it
    too. Once it exited, what it left behind holding the pipe gets STREAM_GRACE seconds, then the pipe is closed. When the Limit waits for the
    check to start (begins_at), `pre_start` is a FIFO evidence-wrap reads after the check ended (pre_start_fifo): the seconds it waited are
    written to it then (answer_pre_start), or nothing when it never started."""
    sys.stdout.flush()
    raw = getattr(sys.stdout, 'buffer', None)
    decode = codecs.getincrementaldecoder('utf-8')(errors='replace').decode
    limit = timeout if isinstance(timeout, Limit) else Limit(timeout)

    def take(chunk):
        kept.write(chunk)
        limit.output(chunk)
        if raw:
            raw.write(chunk)
            raw.flush()
        else:
            sys.stdout.write(decode(chunk))
            sys.stdout.flush()

    with subprocess.Popen(argv, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, **options) as process, open(log, 'wb') as kept:
        try:
            pipe, ended, answered = process.stdout.fileno(), None, pre_start is None
            while limit.remaining() > 0 and (ended is None or time.monotonic() - ended < STREAM_GRACE):
                if ended is None and process.poll() is not None:
                    ended = time.monotonic()
                if not answered:
                    answered = answer_pre_start(pre_start, pipe, take, limit)
                if not select.select([pipe], [], [], 0.2)[0]:
                    continue
                chunk = os.read(pipe, 65536)
                if not chunk:
                    break
                take(chunk)
            return wait_limited(process, limit)
        except BaseException:
            process.kill()
            raise


def target_warm(target):
    """Whether the cargo target a build check uses already held something before this run (after a trim, which empties it): a warm run. A
    tree's own target (ST_EVIDENCE_TARGET=off) is never warm, because the tree is cleaned before every run."""
    target = target or (Path(os.environ['CARGO_TARGET_DIR']) if os.environ.get('CARGO_TARGET_DIR') else None)
    try:
        return target is not None and any(target.iterdir())
    except OSError:
        return False


def runs_path():
    """Where each run's times are appended (ST_TEST_RUNS, read before the seat scrub like the other ST_TEST_* settings), or None for `off`."""
    value = POOL_SETTINGS.get('ST_TEST_RUNS', '')
    if value == 'off':
        return None
    if value:
        return Path(value)
    state = os.environ.get('XDG_STATE_HOME', '')
    return Path(state if os.path.isabs(state) else Path.home() / '.local/state') / 'st-test/runs.jsonl'


def record_run(record):
    """Append `record` as one JSON line: one write with O_APPEND, so concurrent runs never interleave. Fail-soft: a file that cannot be
    written is one line on stderr, never a different exit code."""
    path = runs_path()
    if path is None:
        return
    line = (json.dumps(record, separators=(',', ':')) + '\n').encode()
    try:
        path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        fd = os.open(path, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_APPEND | os.O_CREAT, 0o644)
        try:
            written = os.write(fd, line)
        finally:
            os.close(fd)
        if written != len(line):
            raise OSError(f'short write to {path} ({written} of {len(line)} bytes)')
    except OSError as error:
        print(f'fractal-test: could not record this run\'s times: {error}', file=sys.stderr)


def utc(seconds):
    return time.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ', time.gmtime(seconds))


def main(argv):
    main_started = time.monotonic()
    POOL_SETTINGS.clear()
    POOL_SETTINGS.update({name: os.environ[name] for name in SETTING_NAMES if name in os.environ})
    scrub_git_environment()
    scrub_seat_environment()
    # The build host's finish-first admission order: every stage of this run carries one run name and start time, so once
    # the run has had a heavy admission its later stages are served by the run's start instead of queueing behind runs that began after it.
    os.environ['ADMISSION_RUN'] = f'st-test-{os.getpid()}@{time.time_ns()}'
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.split('\n\n')[0])
    parser.add_argument('--check', action='append', default=[], metavar='NAME', help='only this check (repeatable)')
    parser.add_argument('--base', default='origin/main', help='the accepted base (default origin/main)')
    publication = parser.add_mutually_exclusive_group()
    publication.add_argument('--push', metavar='REMOTE', help='push the signed notes here after they verify')
    publication.add_argument('--out', metavar='DIR', help='rerun the selection and export packs here without importing or pushing; verification follows publication')
    parser.add_argument('--repository', metavar='OWNER/NAME', help='default: from the remote\'s GitHub URL; without it the policy is not applied')
    parser.add_argument('--rerun', action='store_true', help='run a check even when this tree already has a passing signature for it')
    parser.add_argument('--crate', action='append', default=[], metavar='NAME', help='with --check rust-tests: only these crates (repeatable)')
    parser.add_argument('--attempt', default='1')
    parser.add_argument('--time-limit', type=int, default=7200, help='seconds each check may run, not counting its waits for a build slot (default 7200)')
    parser.add_argument('--queue-limit', type=int, default=QUEUE_LIMIT,
                        help=f'seconds each check may wait for build slots, apart from --time-limit (default {QUEUE_LIMIT})')
    parser.add_argument('--list', action='store_true', help='print the checks and their commands and stop')
    parser.add_argument('--seed', nargs='?', const='', metavar='REF', help='sign the per-crate rust-tests notes of REF (default: the base) instead of HEAD, '
                        'for the crates that lack one, and push them (to origin unless --push says otherwise); with --out, export every selected crate instead: '
                        'what a merge-queue signer runs after a merge that moved a crate key or the lane, from any checkout, whatever HEAD is')
    parser.add_argument('--all', action='store_true', help='accepted, and the same as no flag: every check runs')
    parser.add_argument('--status', action='store_true', help='run nothing: say whether each check is already covered for this tree (a lane edit, '
                        'and with no --check a policy check that has no local command, count as covered: CI runs them); exit 0 if all are, 3 if not')
    parser.add_argument('--toolchain-sha256', help='passed to evidence-wrap: skip the nix evaluation, record this digest')
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    if args.out is not None:
        args.out = str(Path(args.out).resolve())
        args.rerun = True
    if not re.fullmatch(r'[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9._/-]*', args.base):
        raise Failure(f'--base {args.base!r} must be a branch or ref name (it may not start with "-")')
    unknown = [name for name in args.check if name not in LOCAL]
    if unknown:
        raise Failure(f'no local command for {", ".join(unknown)}; known: {", ".join(LOCAL)}')
    TARGET.clear()
    if args.seed is not None:
        if args.check and args.check != [RUST_TESTS]:
            raise Failure('--seed signs rust-tests only; it takes no other --check')
        resolved = git('rev-parse', '--verify', '-q', (args.seed or args.base) + '^{commit}', check=False)
        if resolved.returncode != 0:
            raise Failure(f'--seed {(args.seed or args.base)!r} is not a commit (fetch it first)')
        TARGET.append(text(resolved))
        args.check = [RUST_TESTS]
        if not args.out:
            args.push = args.push or 'origin'
    checks = args.check or [name for name in LOCAL if name not in OPT_IN]
    global CHECKS
    CHECKS = tuple(checks)
    if args.list:
        for name in checks:
            print(f'{name}: {" ".join(LOCAL[name])}')
        return 0

    # A run's times count from here (the base fetch, the pool tree, the members and the notes fetch are part of what a run costs).
    started_wall, started = time.time(), time.monotonic()
    remote = args.base.partition('/')[0]
    base_commit = fetch_base(args.base)
    if args.status:
        commit, why_merge = gate_commit(args.base)
        if why_merge:
            print(f'fractal-test: {why_merge}')
        tree = text(git('rev-parse', f'{commit}^{{tree}}'))
        lane_base = branch_point(commit, base_commit)
        with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(prefix='st-test-') as scratch:
            verifier = Path(scratch) / 'evidence-verify'
            verifier.write_bytes(git('show', f'{args.base}:scripts/evidence-verify').stdout)
            repository = args.repository or repository_of(remote or 'origin')
            lanes, rows = lane_names(args.base) if edited_lanes_unsigned(args.base) else set(), []
            for name in checks:
                # Against an older base a tree that edits a check's lane cannot be signed (its evidence-wrap refuses it): CI runs that check.
                # Otherwise a lane edit is signed, and asked about, like any other tree.
                edits = lane_edits(str(verifier), Path('.'), lane_base, name, tree) if name in lanes else []
                unchanged = web_unchanged(args.base) if name == WEB else None
                if edits:
                    rows.append((name, True, 'lane edit: CI runs it', f'this tree edits its lane ({", ".join(edits[:3])})'))
                elif name == RUST_TESTS:
                    keys, error = crate_keys(args.base, Path('.'), tree, scratch)
                    if not error:
                        keys, error = only_crates(keys, args.crate)
                    if error:
                        rows.append((name, False, 'not covered', f'cannot compute the per-crate keys: {error}'))
                    else:
                        fetch_crate_notes(args.push or 'origin', keys)
                        missing, have = uncovered_crates(str(verifier), Path('.'), tree, args.base,
                                                         repository if judged_by_policy(args.base) else None, lane_base, keys)
                        rows.append((name, not missing, 'covered' if not missing else f'{len(missing)} crates to run',
                                     f'{have} of {len(keys)} crates covered' + (f'; to run: {", ".join(c for c, _ in missing[:6])}'
                                                                                 + (' ...' if len(missing) > 6 else '') if missing else '')))
                elif unchanged:
                    rows.append((name, True, 'not relevant', unchanged))
                else:
                    is_covered, why = covered(str(verifier), Path('.'), tree, name, args.base, repository, lane_base)
                    rows.append((name, is_covered, 'covered' if is_covered else 'not covered', why))
            if not args.check:
                # Every check of the skip policy: one without a local command cannot be signed here, so CI runs it.
                rows += [(name, True, 'CI runs it', 'no local command') for name in policy_checks(args.base) if name not in LOCAL]
        for name, is_covered, label, why in rows:
            print(f'  {name:<24} {label:<22} {why}')
        return 0 if all(row[1] for row in rows) else 3
    status = git('status', '--porcelain', '--untracked-files=no').stdout.decode().strip()
    if status:
        raise Failure('the checkout has uncommitted changes to tracked files; commit them first, the note is for the committed tree')
    limit = load_limit()
    if limit:
        wait_for_load(limit)
    reap_stale()
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(prefix='st-test-') as scratch:
        os.chmod(scratch, 0o700)
        (Path(scratch) / 'pid').write_text(str(os.getpid()))
        directory = Path(scratch) / 'tree'
        pool_trees, tree_lock, tree_target, n, merged = pool_size(), None, None, None, None
        tree_waits, tree_taken, blocked = {}, None, {}  # the tree's waits, when it was taken, and what the build lane waited holding it
        if not needs_tree(checks):
            pool_trees = None  # a throwaway worktree, as with ST_TEST_TREES=off, and no wait for a tree
        try:
            if pool_trees:
                root = pool_root()
                target_commit(args.base)  # a branch that does not merge with the base fails now, not after the wait for a tree
                # The slots can change while the run waits; a free tree is taken only when the run's build could start (slot first).
                n, tree_lock = acquire_tree(root, lambda: pool_size() or pool_trees, slot_ready=build_slot_ready, waits=tree_waits)
                tree_taken = time.monotonic()
                # The wait for a tree can take hours, and main moves meanwhile: the base is fetched again and the commit to sign chosen at
                # its tip now, so what is signed is only as old as the run itself (2026-10-26: a run that waited 1h50m signed a merge with a
                # main that had already moved a protected file, and the push was refused).
                base_commit = fetch_base(args.base)
                merged = merge_base_in(args.base)
                commit, why_merge = gate_commit(args.base)
                if why_merge:
                    print(f'fractal-test: {why_merge}', flush=True)
                if pool_max_gb():
                    evict_to_cap(pool_base(), pool_max_gb(), keep=(root, n))
                directory, tree_target = prepare_tree(root, n, commit), root / f'target-{n}'
            else:
                merged = merge_base_in(args.base)
                directory, commit = target_worktree(args.base, scratch)
            tools = {}
            for name in BASE_SCRIPTS:  # from the base as it is now, after any wait for a tree
                path = Path(scratch) / name
                path.write_bytes(git('show', f'{args.base}:scripts/{name}').stdout)
                path.chmod(0o700)
                tools[name] = str(path)
            lag = behind(args.base)
            tree = text(git('rev-parse', f'{commit}^{{tree}}', cwd=directory))
            lane_base = branch_point(commit, base_commit)
            repository = args.repository or repository_of(remote or 'origin')
            # -I: the signer's interpreter reads no PYTHON* variable, user site or current directory (evidence-wrap also drops them).
            signer = os.environ.get('EVIDENCE_SIGNER') or json.dumps([sys.executable, '-I', tools['evidence-sign']])
            rows, failed, unsigned = [], False, False
            lanes = lane_names(args.base)
            unsignable = lanes if edited_lanes_unsigned(args.base) else set()  # lanes whose edits the base's wrapper refuses to sign
            target, target_lock = shared_target(tree_target) if any(name in BUILDS for name in checks) else (None, None)
            warm = target_warm(target) if any(name in BUILDS for name in checks) else None  # before anything builds in it
            # The members are fetched once, and only when a check has to run: a tree whose checks are all signed needs none. The light
            # lane runs in a thread, so the first caller fetches under a lock.
            members_lock, members_state = threading.Lock(), []

            def members():
                with members_lock:
                    if not members_state:
                        members_state.append(materialize_members(directory))
                    return members_state[0]

            setup_seconds = round(time.monotonic() - main_started)  # fetch and the clean tree (the members are fetched when a check first needs them)
            gate_report = Path(scratch) / 'linux-gate-nextest-junit.xml'
            stages = []  # linux-gate's stages, from scripts/ci-local's closing table, for the run's record
            def run_one(name, output=None):
                """(row, failed, unsigned) for one check; its output goes to `output` (a file) when given, else to this terminal."""
                edits = lane_edits(tools['evidence-verify'], directory, lane_base, name, tree) if name in unsignable else []
                if edits:
                    return (name, 'not signed', 0, f'this tree edits its lane ({", ".join(edits[:3])}); CI runs it'), False, True
                if name == RUST_TESTS:
                    problem = members()
                    if problem:
                        return (name, 'NOT RUN', 0, problem), True, False
                    # One run, one row for the run and one for each crate that did not sign (a list: the collector below takes either).
                    new_rows, bad = run_rust_tests(args, directory, tools, scratch, tree, commit, base_commit, lane_base, repository, signer, lanes,
                                                   target, report=gate_report)
                    return new_rows, bad, any(row[1] == UNCOVERED for row in new_rows)  # a crate left for CI: exit 4, not 1
                unchanged = web_unchanged(args.base) if name == WEB else None
                if unchanged:
                    return (name, 'not relevant', 0, unchanged), False, False  # nothing to run, nothing to sign: not a failure
                if not args.rerun:
                    # Already signed for this tree (or its input key): nothing to measure again. Asked the way it is asked after a run.
                    signed, why, record = ask_verifier(tools['evidence-verify'], directory, tree, name, args.base, repository, lane_base)
                    if signed:
                        # With --push the covering note must also be on the remote (an earlier run may not have pushed it): push it as it is.
                        failure = push_record(directory, args.push, tree, record) if args.push else None
                        return (name, 'already signed' if not failure else 'push failed', 0, why if not failure else failure), bool(failure), False
                problem = members()
                if problem:
                    return (name, 'NOT RUN', 0, problem), True, False
                wrap = [sys.executable, tools['evidence-wrap'], '--check', name, '--attempt', args.attempt,
                        '--signer', signer, '--verifier', tools['evidence-verify'], '--keys-from', args.base]
                if name in lanes:
                    # The branch point defines this lane: the note carries the digest of its definition, and a tree that edits the lane is not
                    # signed. It is the base itself when the branch holds the base's tip.
                    wrap += ['--lanes-from', lane_base]
                if args.toolchain_sha256:
                    wrap += ['--toolchain-sha256', args.toolchain_sha256]
                if args.push:
                    wrap += ['--push', args.push]
                if args.out:
                    wrap += ['--out', args.out]
                environment = dict(os.environ) if os.environ.get('ST_EVIDENCE_PATH') == 'keep' else dict(os.environ, PATH=check_path())
                environment.setdefault('CARGO_INCREMENTAL', '0')  # as CI builds: a one-shot run gains nothing from incremental state and it is ~40% of the target
                if target and name in BUILDS:
                    environment['CARGO_TARGET_DIR'] = str(target)
                # The nix wrapper admits a `nix develop`/`nix shell` start as heavy unless told the shell compiles no Rust: a check that
                # builds nothing starts as light (as the pre-sign worker starts .#web and .#genie), and one that builds never does.
                if name in BUILDS:
                    environment.pop('BUILD_ADMISSION_CLASS', None)
                else:
                    environment['BUILD_ADMISSION_CLASS'] = 'light'
                if name == 'linux-gate' and RUST_TESTS in checks and 'tests' in LOCAL[name]:
                    # rust-tests signs from this run's own nextest report (same build, whole selection): ask the tests stage to write it.
                    environment['CI_TEST_JUNIT'] = str(gate_report)
                if name in BUILDS:
                    # A test that cannot run on this host (no unprivileged user namespace, say) skips outside CI and writes its id here;
                    # the note records the list as env_skips, so the evidence says which tests did not run on this machine. rust-tests
                    # reads linux-gate's list for its per-crate records, so it is written even under a base wrapper without --input-file.
                    skips = env_skips_file(scratch, name)
                    environment['CI_TEST_ENV_SKIPS'] = str(skips)
                    if takes_input_files(tools['evidence-wrap']):
                        wrap += ['--input-file', f'env_skips={skips}']
                if name == 'linux-gate' and 'tests' in LOCAL[name] and takes_input_files(tools['evidence-wrap']):
                    # The tests that passed only on a retry (scripts/ci-nextest-flaky writes them after the tests stage): the whole-tree note
                    # still signs, as CI's gate counts a retried pass, and records them as flaky_tests so the evidence says so. rust-tests
                    # does not sign a crate with one (run_rust_tests), as CI's own per-crate notes do not.
                    flaky = Path(scratch) / 'flaky-linux-gate.txt'
                    environment['CI_TEST_FLAKY'] = str(flaky)
                    wrap += ['--input-file', f'flaky_tests={flaky}']
                if name in BASE_BOUND:
                    # clippy's ratchet compares with the base, as CI compares with the event's base: the base as just fetched,
                    # given to the stage and recorded in the note, so the base it was measured against is part of what is signed.
                    wrap += ['--input', f'clippy_base_sha={base_commit}']
                    environment['CLIPPY_BASE_SHA'] = base_commit
                if name == WEB:
                    wrap += [argument for key, value in WEB_INPUTS.items() for argument in ('--input', f'{key}={value}')]
                started = time.monotonic()
                streams = output is None and CI_LOCAL in LOCAL[name]
                limit = Limit(args.time_limit, Path(scratch) / f'{name}.queue', args.queue_limit, begins_at=CI_LOCAL_STARTED if streams else None)
                environment['CI_LOCAL_QUEUE_LOG'] = str(limit.queue_log)
                pre_start = None
                if streams and takes_input_files(tools['evidence-wrap']):
                    # The seconds it waited before ci-local started, recorded in the note: a FIFO the wrapper reads after the check ended,
                    # answered once it does (answer_pre_start).
                    pre_start = pre_start_fifo(Path(scratch) / f'pre-start-{name}.txt')
                    wrap += ['--input-file', f'pre_start_s={pre_start}']
                command = wrap + ['--'] + LOCAL[name]
                print(f'fractal-test: evidence-wrap argv: {shlex.join(command)}', file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
                command = governed(command, name) if name in BUILDS else command
                if streams:
                    # scripts/ci-local ends with a table of its stages' times: its output still reaches this terminal as it comes, and is kept
                    # so the run's record can carry the stages.
                    log = Path(scratch) / f'{name}-output.log'
                    code = streamed(command, log, limit, cwd=directory, env=environment, pre_start=pre_start)
                    stages[:] = ci_local_stages(log)
                    blocked[name] = (round(limit.waiting()), round(blocked_seconds(limit.queue_log, stages)))
                else:
                    code = run_limited(command, limit, cwd=directory, env=environment, stdout=output,
                                       stderr=subprocess.STDOUT if output else None)
                seconds = round(time.monotonic() - started)
                ok = code == 0
                if args.out:
                    why = 'packs exported, not imported or verified' if ok else 'export failed'
                else:
                    is_covered, why = covered(tools['evidence-verify'], directory, tree, name, args.base, repository, lane_base)
                return (name, 'passed' if ok else ('timed out' if code == 124 else f'FAILED ({code})'), seconds, why + queue_note(limit)), not ok, False

            # The light checks run alongside the ones that build, one after another in a lane of their own, as CI runs them on runners of
            # their own: they wait on network, Nix and Node rather than the Rust build, and run after it they added minutes to every
            # run. Their output is kept and printed in order once the build lane is done, so the two never interleave. ST_EVIDENCE_SERIAL=1
            # runs every check one after another, as before.
            builds = [name for name in checks if name in BUILDS]
            light = [name for name in checks if name not in BUILDS]
            results = {}
            parallel = bool(builds and light and os.environ.get('ST_EVIDENCE_SERIAL') != '1')
            if parallel:
                logs = {name: Path(scratch) / f'{name}.log' for name in light}

                def light_lane():
                    for name in light:
                        with open(logs[name], 'w') as output:
                            try:
                                results[name] = run_one(name, output)
                            except Exception as error:  # a lane that dies is a failed check, never a silent pass
                                results[name] = ((name, f'FAILED ({type(error).__name__})', 0, str(error)[:120]), True, False)

                lane = threading.Thread(target=light_lane, name='st-test-light')
                print(f'fractal-test: {", ".join(light)} run alongside {", ".join(builds)}; their output follows when it is done', flush=True)
                lane.start()
                for name in builds:
                    results[name] = run_one(name)
                lane.join()
                for name in light:
                    print(f'\n----- fractal-test: {name} output -----', flush=True)
                    sys.stdout.write(logs[name].read_text(errors='replace') if logs[name].exists() else '(no output)\n')
                    sys.stdout.flush()
            else:
                for name in checks:
                    results[name] = run_one(name)
            for name in checks:
                row, check_failed, check_unsigned = results[name]
                rows += row if isinstance(row, list) else [row]
                failed = failed or check_failed
                unsigned = unsigned or check_unsigned
            print(f'\nfractal-test: tree {tree[:12]} (HEAD{f", {lag} commit(s) behind {args.base}: the queue tests the merge" if lag else ""}), in a clean worktree')
            rows.insert(0, ('setup', 'done', setup_seconds, 'fetch and the clean tree (the megarepo members are fetched when a check first needs them)'))
            for name, result, seconds, why in rows:
                print(f'  {name:<20} {result:<12} {seconds:>6}s  {why}')
            if tree_target and target and target == tree_target and target.exists():
                print(f'  target-{n}: {du_gb(target):.1f} GB (emptied before a run past {TARGET_MAX_GB:g} GB; the pool is capped at the sum of all, '
                      f'ST_TEST_POOL_MAX_GB)')
            if args.push and any(result != 'NOT RUN' for _, result, _, _ in rows):
                line = nudge_log()
                if line:
                    print(f'  {line}')
            code = 1 if failed else (4 if unsigned else 0)
            held = {}
            if tree_taken is not None:
                # What the run's tree did for it: how long the run waited for it and held it, and how much of that the build lane spent
                # blocked in it (before ci-local started: build admission and the dev shell; then every stage waiting for a build slot).
                pre, slots = (sum(values) for values in zip(*blocked.values())) if blocked else (0, 0)
                held = {'tree_wait_s': tree_waits.get('tree_wait_s'), 'tree_slot_wait_s': tree_waits.get('slot_wait_s'),
                        'tree_held_s': round(time.monotonic() - tree_taken), 'tree_blocked_s': pre + slots, 'tree_pre_start_s': pre,
                        'tree_slot_blocked_s': slots}
                print(f'  tree-{n}: waited {held["tree_wait_s"]}s for it ({held["tree_slot_wait_s"]}s of that free, waiting for a build slot), held it '
                      f'{held["tree_held_s"]}s, blocked in it {held["tree_blocked_s"]}s ({pre}s before ci-local started: admission, dev shell; '
                      f'{slots}s with every build stage waiting for a slot)')
            # One line per run for the time objective (scripts/fractal-test-report): only a run of the whole suite (no --check, no --crate) counts.
            record_run({'v': 1, 'started': utc(started_wall), 'ended': utc(time.time()), 'total_s': round(time.monotonic() - started),
                        'host': socket.gethostname().split('.')[0], 'tree': tree, 'commit': commit, 'base': base_commit, 'behind': lag,
                        'full': not args.check and not args.crate, 'checks': checks, 'pool_tree': n, 'warm': warm,
                        'parallel': parallel, 'rows': [{'name': name, 'result': result, 'seconds': seconds} for name, result, seconds, _ in rows],
                        'exit': code, 'stages': stages, **held})
            if merged and args.push and code == 0:
                push_branch(args.push, args.base, merged)
            elif merged:
                print(f'fractal-test: the merge {merged[:12]} is on the branch here, not pushed' + (f' (exit {code})' if code else ''), flush=True)
            return code
        finally:
            if not pool_trees and directory.exists():
                git('worktree', 'remove', '--force', str(directory), check=False)
            if tree_lock:
                os.utime(tree_lock.name)  # last used: now, for the eviction order
                tree_lock.close()


if __name__ == '__main__':
    # TERM (the pre-push hook's timeout, a closed terminal) unwinds like an error so the throwaway worktree is removed.
    signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, lambda signum, frame: sys.exit(143))
    if os.environ.get(REEXEC) and Path(__file__).parent.name.startswith('st-test-base-'):
        atexit.register(shutil.rmtree, Path(__file__).parent, True)  # the copy run_base_copy wrote: gone when this run ends
    try:
        run_base_copy(sys.argv[1:])
        sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
    except Failure as error:
        print(f'fractal-test: {error}', file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(2)
